WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:08.480
On the 26th of April 1923, Prince Albert,&nbsp;
Duke of York, married Elizabeth Bowes-Lyon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:15.040
at Westminster Abbey. He was the younger&nbsp;
son of King George V of the United Kingdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:22.400
and she came from a largely Scottish noble&nbsp;
family. Albert, known to his family as Bertie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:29.840
was not supposed to become a king and Elizabeth&nbsp;
was never meant to be a queen. That duty was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:36.560
intended to fall to Bertie’s older brother Edward.&nbsp;
Had he been the heir to the throne, it is likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:44.160
Albert’s parents would have pushed him to marry a&nbsp;
daughter of one of Europe’s royal households. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:53.600
fate would align in strange ways. Thirteen years&nbsp;
later Albert’s older brother briefly became king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:04.240
in January 1936 and abdicated in December. Thus,&nbsp;
Bertie was thrust into taking up a crown he never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:11.600
wanted. It was only then that the wisdom of&nbsp;
his marriage became clear. As King George VI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:19.520
Bertie relied greatly on his wife to overcome&nbsp;
his own natural shyness and reserved character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.920
With Elizabeth by his side, his reign was&nbsp;
a successful one, leading Britain through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:33.040
the Second World War and the shifting position&nbsp;
of the monarchy in its aftermath. This is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:44.720
story of King George VI and the Queen Mother, the&nbsp;
unlikely king and queen of the United Kingdom. 
&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:56.400
The man known to history as King George VI of&nbsp;
Britain was born as Albert Frederick Arthur George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:05.120
on the 14th of December 1895 at York Cottage on&nbsp;
the Sandringham Estate in Norfolk, England. 
&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:12.560
His father was Prince George, Duke of York, a&nbsp;
grandson of Queen Victoria, who at the time of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:20.240
Albert’s birth was nearing the end of her sixth&nbsp;
decade on the throne of Britain. She was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:27.920
the first Empress of India and ruled the vast&nbsp;
British overseas empire, on which it was said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:35.840
the sun never set. Until shortly before Albert’s&nbsp;
birth, Prince George had been out of the direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:43.360
line of succession to the throne. Once Victoria&nbsp;
died, George’s father, Albert, Prince of Wales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:50.800
would become king. But it had been assumed until&nbsp;
the early 1890s that George’s older brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:59.120
Albert Victor, as Victoria’s eldest male heir,&nbsp;
would ascend to the throne in due course. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:08.000
Albert Victor died prematurely in 1892, ensuring&nbsp;
that the future George VI’s father became second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:17.600
in line to the throne from 1892 onwards. Thus,&nbsp;
Albert was born in 1895 into a household which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:24.960
would someday most likely constitute Britain’s&nbsp;
immediate royal family with his father as king and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:32.960
his mother as queen consort. However, Albert was&nbsp;
not his father’s heir. An older brother, Edward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:41.440
had been born in the summer of 1894, a year and a&nbsp;
half before Albert and Edward was third in line to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:49.840
the throne. Consequently, from the moment he was&nbsp;
born in the winter of 1895, Albert was the fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:56.160
in line to the throne of Britain, though he would&nbsp;
only succeed to that position should something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:03.280
ever happen to displace his older brother&nbsp;
Edward. As we shall see, something did occur. 
&nbsp;

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:11.520
Albert’s mother was Mary of Teck, a member of the&nbsp;
German royal house of Teck which held extensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:19.040
estates in the unified German Empire. Albert&nbsp;
was her and George’s second child after Edward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:30.640
Four more children would follow, Mary in 1897,&nbsp;
Henry in 1900, George in 1902 and John in 1905,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:39.040
though John suffered from severe epilepsy from&nbsp;
which he would die in 1919 when only 13 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:45.280
of age. Albert, who quickly became known&nbsp;
to his family as ‘Bertie’, the same name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:53.120
given to his grandfather, was baptised at St Mary&nbsp;
Magdalene Church in Sandringham just a few weeks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:05:00.000
after he was born. Thereafter he was largely&nbsp;
reared in a separate household to his parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.160
an entirely normal practice amongst the royal&nbsp;
families of Europe in the nineteenth century.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:12.880
This continued through his early childhood years,&nbsp;
during which Albert, Edward and their growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:19.920
brood of siblings were chaperoned between royal&nbsp;
palaces and cottages, taught by tutors in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:26.560
standard subjects of the Victorian educational&nbsp;
curriculum, which in those days still involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:33.600
learning Latin and had a strong focus on the&nbsp;
classics of ancient Greek and Roman literature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:40.560
Albert’s parents were distant figures, who some&nbsp;
historians and observers have since deemed to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:48.240
been neglectful. This is too harsh an assessment&nbsp;
and if they seemed to be cold parents it was in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:55.200
line with the conventions of the time. Albert’s&nbsp;
father was also a strict disciplinarian. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.200 --> 00:06:01.040
perhaps on account of the traumatic elements of&nbsp;
his youth that he began to suffer from a stutter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:08.880
in his younger years, one which would continue to&nbsp;
plague him into adulthood, though as we will see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:15.680
he largely triumphed over it in his&nbsp;
thirties, well before he became king. 
&nbsp;

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:23.280
When he was just 14 years old, Albert was&nbsp;
sent to the Royal Naval College at Osborne on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:30.000
the Isle of Wight, a training school for royals&nbsp;
and sons of the British aristocracy to train as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:37.520
officer cadets. This followed a well-established&nbsp;
tradition and Albert’s father had also been sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:44.960
to join the British Royal Navy when he was barely&nbsp;
a teenager. Albert, it must be said, was not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:52.640
great student of any kind. He came bottom of his&nbsp;
class in the cadets’ final exam at Osborne, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.640 --> 00:07:00.320
he was physically not predisposed to seafaring,&nbsp;
having suffered from stomach issues as a youth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:08.720
His confidence was also low in his younger years,&nbsp;
in part owing to his stutter and also because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:15.600
having been forced to learn to write with his&nbsp;
right hand, even though he was left-handed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:22.560
Although it seems nonsensical to the modern mind,&nbsp;
this was a common feature of schooling in the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:29.840
nineteenth and early twentieth centuries. It was&nbsp;
also while he was at Osborne that his grandfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:37.360
King Edward VII, died. With this his father&nbsp;
ascended the throne as King George V and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:44.400
Albert’s older brother Edward became the Prince&nbsp;
of Wales and heir to the throne. Albert was now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:50.640
second in line to the throne, though something&nbsp;
unexpected would need to befall Edward for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:58.960
to ever become king. Meanwhile, in the early 1910s&nbsp;
he continued to progress through the Royal Navy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:05.200
joining the Royal Naval College at Dartmouth&nbsp;
after his sojourn at Osborne and then taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:13.040
in several training tours in 1912 and 1913,&nbsp;
voyages which saw him traversing much of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.040 --> 00:08:21.360
Atlantic in the Caribbean and off the seaboard&nbsp;
of North America. In late 1913 he was finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:30.000
posted to the HMS Collingwood as a midshipman. 
Albert was still struggling to find his sea legs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.920
an occupational hazard for a mariner, as&nbsp;
diplomatic tensions were building in Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:44.480
in 1914. For decades the continent’s great powers&nbsp;
had been engaged in ongoing rivalries for regional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:52.000
power in Europe and for possession of colonies&nbsp;
overseas in Africa and southern Asia. Russia and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:58.800
Britain, for instance, had been rivals for a time&nbsp;
in Central Asia where they both had interests in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:05.520
countries like Afghanistan. The French and the&nbsp;
Italians both had interests in North Africa and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:12.720
the Horn of Africa. Since the 1890s Germany,&nbsp;
which had emerged as a major power on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:20.960
continent following unification in 1871, began&nbsp;
trying to build its own overseas empire. Armed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:29.360
alliances had even developed, with Britain, France&nbsp;
and Russia forming the Triple Entente and Germany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:36.640
having a long-standing alliance with the Empire&nbsp;
of Austria-Hungary. Yet despite these rivalries, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:43.760
major conflict had been avoided for many years. As&nbsp;
a result, when diplomatic tensions began brewing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:52.000
between Austria-Hungary, Russia and Serbia in&nbsp;
the Balkans in July 1914 many believed that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.000 --> 00:10:00.560
crisis, like many before it, would pass quickly.&nbsp;
It did not, and in the final days of July tensions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:08.080
escalated rapidly, leading to a succession of&nbsp;
declarations of war. By early August nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:16.000
every country in Europe had committed to one side&nbsp;
or another as Britain, France and Russia went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:25.280
war with Germany, Austria-Hungary and the Ottoman&nbsp;
Empire. The First World War had commenced. 
&nbsp;

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:30.640
In the early stages of it, Albert was&nbsp;
fighting another kind of conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:37.760
one with his appendix. In late August a medical&nbsp;
evaluation determined that he needed to have his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:45.200
operated upon and when his ship made port in the&nbsp;
Scottish city of Aberdeen it was removed. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:52.080
a sufficient period of rest and convalescence he&nbsp;
returned to service on board the HMS Collingwood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:58.160
The ship spent most of the war stationed in the&nbsp;
North Sea patrolling the vast waters between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:05.360
Britain north to Iceland and east towards Norway.&nbsp;
While Britain was the pre-eminent naval power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:11.440
the day and had been so for two centuries,&nbsp;
the Germans had spent an enormous amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.440 --> 00:11:18.880
money building a sizeable navy in the ten or so&nbsp;
years leading up to the war. Accordingly there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:25.360
was an expectation that major naval engagements&nbsp;
would occur in the North Atlantic before long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.360 --> 00:11:32.400
but in the end the war at sea was very limited&nbsp;
by comparison with the carnage occurring in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:39.840
trenches of the Western Front in France.&nbsp;
Therefore Albert spent much of late 1914,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:48.720
all of 1915 and into early 1916 on board the&nbsp;
Collingwood undertaking gunnery drills and patrols&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:55.840
in the waters north of Scotland, but seeing&nbsp;
little active engagement with the enemy. 
&nbsp;

00:11:55.840 --> 00:12:02.320
Albert was present for the largest naval clash&nbsp;
between Britain and Germany during the war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:07.440
The Battle of Jutland took place over&nbsp;
the course of the 31st of May and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:14.960
1st of June 1916 in the waters off the coast&nbsp;
of western Denmark and north-western Germany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:20.880
as both sides sought to score a tactical&nbsp;
breakthrough at sea which might turn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:28.480
course of the war. The British had the greater&nbsp;
number of ships, with just over 150 vessels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:35.600
28 of them being the Dreadnought battleships, the&nbsp;
foremost military vessel of the day, supplemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:42.560
by nearly eighty destroyer class ships. The&nbsp;
German armada was just under a hundred ships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:51.360
with just 16 Dreadnoughts. Over 60% of its vessels&nbsp;
were torpedo boats and the German attack would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:57.520
rely on these scoring a number of hits before&nbsp;
they ran out of torpedoes in order for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:05.840
Germans to emerge out of the clash victorious. In&nbsp;
the ensuing naval melee Albert served as a junior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:12.000
officer aboard the HMS Collingwood. He performed&nbsp;
well during the battle and was mentioned as such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:19.040
in the dispatches, but the battle was a mixed&nbsp;
affair overall. As the British and German fleets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:26.160
engaged with each other across a large stretch&nbsp;
of sea, the Germans ultimately scored more hits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:34.400
sinking 14 ships while only losing 11, while the&nbsp;
British also lost a disproportionately higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:42.480
number of destroyers and larger battleships and&nbsp;
over twice as many mariners. As such, the Germans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:49.520
statistically won the Battle of Jutland, but it&nbsp;
was a Pyrrhic victory, one in which the Germans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:57.680
lost vital naval resources. In its aftermath&nbsp;
Berlin decided to prioritise submarine warfare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.680 --> 00:14:05.360
and there would be no second major naval clash of&nbsp;
this kind again during the First World War. 
&nbsp;

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:12.320
Albert would spend much of the war away from&nbsp;
active service, in large part owing to renewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:20.720
ill health. Early in 1917 he began suffering from&nbsp;
a duodenal ulcer and he would eventually have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:27.280
have this operated on early that winter. When he&nbsp;
returned to duty it was as part of the burgeoning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:35.440
RAF, the Royal Air Force, which was formed on&nbsp;
the 1st of April 1918 as the first independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:42.640
air force operated by any nation anywhere in&nbsp;
the world, a sign of how air warfare had become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.640 --> 00:14:49.120
a central component of military conflict in the&nbsp;
course of the war, where at its outset planes had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:55.040
been used almost exclusively for reconnaissance&nbsp;
missions. As a result of this decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.040 --> 00:15:01.360
Albert became the first member of the British&nbsp;
royal family to hold a pilot’s licence, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:09.440
in October 1918 he would fly over the English&nbsp;
Channel after being posted to France. The newly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:16.960
created RAF only had a limited role to play in&nbsp;
the war in the end, though. By the summer of 1918&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:24.000
the trajectory of the war was clear. The entry of&nbsp;
the United States into the conflict on the side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:29.760
Britain and France the previous year had brought&nbsp;
an insurmountable amount of resources to bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:36.960
against Germany, Austria-Hungary and the Ottomans.&nbsp;
In the end, before victory was won on the field of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:43.120
battle, political unrest across Central Europe&nbsp;
brought about the collapse of the German and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:50.800
Austro-Hungarian empires, bringing the war to an&nbsp;
end in November 1918. 
	
&nbsp;

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:57.680
In the aftermath of the war Bertie returned to&nbsp;
land and civilian life. He began studying at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:05.680
Cambridge University in the autumn of 1919. He was&nbsp;
23 years of age commencing his time in college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:12.960
but this was not unusual in the post-war years&nbsp;
when many freshman students were young men heading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:19.360
towards their mid-twenties who had spent their&nbsp;
late teens and early twenties in the trenches in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:26.640
France. He began attending Trinity College there&nbsp;
alongside his brother, Prince Henry, who was four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:33.600
years his junior. Albert chose to study history&nbsp;
primarily and was tutored by Reginald Laurence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:39.760
the editor of the Cambridge Modern History and&nbsp;
an expert on both ecclesiastical history and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:45.920
French Revolution, though the most substantial&nbsp;
scholar to teach Albert at this time was Dennis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:52.400
Robertson, an economic historian and close&nbsp;
colleague of John Maynard Keynes, the founder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.400 --> 00:16:59.600
of the Keynesian economic theory. At Cambridge&nbsp;
Louis Greig, who Albert had none since his days at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:07.120
Osborne a decade earlier, was employed as Bertie’s&nbsp;
equerry or royal assistant. They developed a keen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:12.960
friendship over their shared interest in tennis&nbsp;
and the pair would later play together at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:19.440
Championships at Wimbledon. Albert’s time at&nbsp;
Cambridge, though, was cut short after just three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:27.600
terms as he was increasingly drawn into becoming&nbsp;
a working royal in the early 1920s, spending much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.600 --> 00:17:35.680
of his time from 1920 onwards visiting industrial&nbsp;
factories and mines across England as the monarchy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:42.160
sought to establish closer ties to the working&nbsp;
classes in Britain at a time when radical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:50.640
socialism was on the front foot across Europe. 
Because he was the second son of the king and at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:56.800
a time when premature death was beginning to&nbsp;
decline dramatically, it was expected in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.800 --> 00:18:04.640
1910s and 1920s that Albert would never be King&nbsp;
of Britain. Therefore he was given something of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:10.800
a free hand to choose his own marriage partner,&nbsp;
a relatively novel development for a monarch’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.800 --> 00:18:17.200
child. Had he been born in the nineteenth century,&nbsp;
for instance, a marriage to a daughter of one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:23.040
Europe’s royal households would most likely&nbsp;
have been arranged. Nevertheless, when Albert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:31.200
began an affair in 1919 with Sheila Chisholm it&nbsp;
aroused consternation in the royal establishment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.200 --> 00:18:37.920
This Australian ‘it-girl’ of the 1910s was&nbsp;
already married to Francis St Clair-Erskine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:44.800
Lord Loughborough. Bertie met Sheila after his&nbsp;
older brother Edward began seeing Chisholm’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:52.480
best friend, Freda Dudley Ward. The relationship&nbsp;
dragged on for almost a year before King George,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.480 --> 00:19:00.480
exasperated by the situation instructed Bertie to&nbsp;
leave this, quote, “already married Australian”.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:07.600
Albert was not happy with doing so, but obeyed his&nbsp;
father’s command. His brother’s unwillingness to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:14.400
abide by a similar injunction from the king over&nbsp;
a decade later would have striking consequences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:21.040
for both Edward and Albert in the long run.&nbsp;
In the shorter term Albert was compensated for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:28.720
ending his affair with Lady Loughborough by being&nbsp;
invested with the title of Duke of York in 1920,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:35.840
one of the most historically significant peerages&nbsp;
in British history and one which had been vacant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:41.920
since his father abandoned the title upon becoming&nbsp;
king in 1910. 
	
&nbsp;

00:19:41.920 --> 00:19:48.240
Bertie’s attentions were soon drawn elsewhere in&nbsp;
his quest for a marriage partner. Shortly after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.240 --> 00:19:55.280
ending his relationship with Lady Loughborough,&nbsp;
he met Lady Elizabeth Bowes-Lyon at an engagement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.280 --> 00:20:01.440
They had known each other as children, but had&nbsp;
not crossed paths in several years. By the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:08.640
they met again Elizabeth was just entering her&nbsp;
twenties and Albert, by then in his mid-twenties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:16.160
was evidently smitten. He proposed in&nbsp;
1921, but Elizabeth turned down his offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:21.760
fearful that entering the royal family and the&nbsp;
public gaze that came with it would result in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:28.480
her being stifled and unable to express her&nbsp;
true self in years to come. Bertie, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.480 --> 00:20:35.440
would not take no for an answer and was determined&nbsp;
to woo her. A second marriage proposal came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:43.680
following Albert’s sister Mary’s wedding to the&nbsp;
heir to the Viscount Lascelles in February 1922,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:51.200
at which Elizabeth had acted as a bridesmaid. She&nbsp;
again said no, but further months of courtship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:58.720
evidently swayed her and in January 1923,&nbsp;
despite her reservations about entering the royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.720 --> 00:21:06.720
establishment, she said yes to Albert on his third&nbsp;
time of asking. The wedding was swiftly organised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:14.240
and the couple were married at Westminster Abbey&nbsp;
in London on the 26th of April 1923. Thereafter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:20.160
they proceeded on their honeymoon, at the start&nbsp;
of which Elizabeth contracted whooping cough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.160 --> 00:21:27.520
in what she later called a thoroughly unromantic&nbsp;
development. Despite this inauspicious beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:33.920
the marriage was to be a notably happy one by&nbsp;
the standards of many royal unions and Albert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:40.640
and Elizabeth had a genuine affection for&nbsp;
one another. It was in many ways the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:47.040
modern royal marriage in British history. 
While the honeymoon might have been interrupted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:53.840
by a bout of whooping cough, there was inevitably&nbsp;
a longer diplomatic tour to be undertaken by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.840 --> 00:22:00.240
couple following their marriage. It was typical&nbsp;
for newlywed senior royals at this time to tour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:07.280
the British Empire so that in an age before&nbsp;
television the people of India, Canada and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:13.280
many other parts of Britain’s dominions could have&nbsp;
an opportunity to see the new member of the royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:20.960
family. This commenced with a visit to Northern&nbsp;
Ireland in July 1924, no doubt in an effort to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:27.200
reassure the Unionist community there of crown&nbsp;
support for their continued presence within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.200 --> 00:22:33.680
United Kingdom following the establishment of the&nbsp;
Irish Free State on the rest of the island during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:41.600
the early 1920s. A tour of Britain’s colonies&nbsp;
in Africa followed, taking in Kenya, Uganda and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.600 --> 00:22:48.720
Sudan, as well as Aden in the south of the Arabian&nbsp;
Peninsula, though the Duke and Duchess of York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:55.840
avoided Egypt where the British Governor-General,&nbsp;
Sir Lee Stack, had just been assassinated on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.840 --> 00:23:03.200
streets of Cairo in November 1924. They returned&nbsp;
to England for a time thereafter in order for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:11.840
Elizabeth to give birth to their first child in&nbsp;
1926, a daughter named Elizabeth after her mother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:19.200
She was the first of their two children, with&nbsp;
another girl named Margaret following in 1930. 
&nbsp;

00:23:19.840 --> 00:23:27.440
As soon as Elizabeth was born in 1926 and her&nbsp;
mother had recovered, the Duke and Duchess resumed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.440 --> 00:23:35.840
their tour of Britain’s overseas colonies. In 1927&nbsp;
they headed west across the Atlantic. They first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:42.400
visited Jamaica, where Albert notably played a&nbsp;
doubles tennis match alongside Bertrand Clark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:49.280
an all-round sporting figure who had competed&nbsp;
internationally in golf, tennis and cricket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:56.080
In 1924 Clark had become the first black athlete&nbsp;
to compete at the Wimbledon tennis Championships&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:56.080 --> 00:24:04.240
in London, a tournament which Albert had himself&nbsp;
competed at in 1926, partnering his friend and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.240 --> 00:24:10.960
mentor Louis Greig, the Scottish naval surgeon&nbsp;
who had served as his equerry at Cambridge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:17.120
in the men’s doubles event. Admittedly they&nbsp;
were soundly beaten in the first round but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:22.400
Albert remains the only British royal&nbsp;
to have competed at the Championships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:28.560
having done so when the Championships were&nbsp;
still an amateur event. Albert’s decision to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:35.280
play alongside Clark in Jamaica the following year&nbsp;
was seen as an inclusive decision which embraced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:35.280 --> 00:24:41.440
the wider Jamaican population. It was probably&nbsp;
simply more in line with Albert’s personality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:46.800
that he innocently decided to play a game of&nbsp;
tennis and wasn’t considering the political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:54.560
overtones of doing so at all. Thereafter, he and&nbsp;
Elizabeth proceeded onwards to the Pacific Ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.560 --> 00:25:02.160
visiting Fiji, New Zealand and Australia, before&nbsp;
returning to Britain after taking in many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:10.400
empire’s countries in the mid-1920s.  
While in Australia Albert oversaw the formal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.400 --> 00:25:16.160
opening of the newly built Parliament House in&nbsp;
the capital city, Canberra. He delivered a speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:22.480
during this event, one which was well delivered.&nbsp;
This would not have been possible just a year or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:30.080
two earlier. Bertie’s stutter had not retreated&nbsp;
with the passage of the years and by the mid-1920s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:35.840
had become a problem. When he had given the&nbsp;
closing speech at the British Empire Exhibition at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.840 --> 00:25:44.640
Wembley in October 1925, the ceremony had been an&nbsp;
endurance test for both Albert and his listeners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:51.040
with the Duke struggling to deliver his lines.&nbsp;
In its aftermath he determined to do something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:57.600
to confront the stutter which had plagued him&nbsp;
since his youth. Thus, although the acclaimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.600 --> 00:26:04.560
film The King’s Speech, depicts Albert as having&nbsp;
employed him much later in the lead up to and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:12.640
opening stages of the Second World War, it was&nbsp;
actually in 1926 that Bertie first began working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:19.760
with Lionel Logue, an Australian former stage&nbsp;
actor turned speech and language therapist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:26.560
Logue’s methods were unusual by the standards&nbsp;
of the 1920s and he was considered a quack by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:33.840
many in the medical community, but his regimen of&nbsp;
daily vocal exercises and conscious relaxing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:40.720
the throat muscles proved enormously successful&nbsp;
in Albert’s case. Already when he had opened the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:40.720 --> 00:26:48.240
Parliament House in Canberra in 1927 the Duke’s&nbsp;
speech was much improved and his voice did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:48.240 --> 00:26:53.280
falter on that occasion. He continued to&nbsp;
work with Logue intermittently over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.280 --> 00:27:01.200
next twenty years and in 1937, at the time of his&nbsp;
coronation, he honoured the Australian by making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.200 --> 00:27:08.240
him a Member of the Royal Victorian Order, with&nbsp;
promotion to the rank of Commander in 1944. 
&nbsp;

00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:16.240
More broadly, Albert grew into himself in&nbsp;
the 1920s. He was a changed man following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.240 --> 00:27:22.480
his marriage and after becoming a father&nbsp;
and unlike his own father and grandfather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:29.280
his parenting style was a warm, modern one,&nbsp;
rather than being a cold, distant presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:36.000
in his daughters’ lives. The family originally&nbsp;
lived at White Lodge in Richmond Park in London,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:43.600
but they moved to a more modest home in Piccadilly&nbsp;
in 1926. During these years the Duke and Duchess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.600 --> 00:27:50.160
became known for their philanthropy. Bertie, for&nbsp;
instance, founded the Industrial Welfare Society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:55.600
through which he met with trade unionists and&nbsp;
other leaders of industrial workers to try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:55.600 --> 00:28:02.080
gain a greater understanding of the material&nbsp;
existences of Britain’s workers and how their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:08.640
lot could be improved at a time when industrial&nbsp;
communities in much of England and Scotland still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:15.040
suffered from striking deprivation. Bertie&nbsp;
became known as ‘the Foreman’ to his family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.040 --> 00:28:21.600
such was his interest in labour issues. He also&nbsp;
established the Duke of York’s Camps through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:27.200
which boys from working class communities&nbsp;
and public schools competed in a wide range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:27.200 --> 00:28:33.360
of events. These were a forerunner of the Duke&nbsp;
of Edinburgh Awards latterly established by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:40.160
son-in-law. Albert took a great personal interest&nbsp;
in them and attended the camps every year in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:48.560
late 1920s and throughout the 1930s except for&nbsp;
1934 when he was ill. 
	
&nbsp;

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:56.160
In the late 1920s and early 1930s Albert and&nbsp;
Elizabeth must surely have believed that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:56.160 --> 00:29:02.720
lives would continue on the same trajectory as&nbsp;
they had been on since their marriage. They would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.720 --> 00:29:09.200
continue to play prominent roles in representing&nbsp;
the royal family as Duke and Duchess of York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:15.200
but the assumption was there that Bertie’s&nbsp;
older brother Edward would eventually marry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:22.000
become king, produce an heir and the royal line&nbsp;
would continue through his family. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:28.720
by the early 1930s it was imperative for&nbsp;
Edward to marry at some point, as he neared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:34.960
his fortieth year. It was worrying for both the&nbsp;
king and the government to discover in the course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:34.960 --> 00:29:42.480
of the mid-1930s that Edward’s attentions had&nbsp;
actually landed on Wallis Simpson, an American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:42.480 --> 00:29:49.920
divorcee who had come to England following her&nbsp;
marriage in 1928 to Ernest Aldrich Simpson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:58.720
an American businessman with extensive dealings in&nbsp;
England. Edward and she had first met in 1932 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:58.720 --> 00:30:06.720
gradually entered into an extra-marital affair. By&nbsp;
1935 when King George sanctioned the Metropolitan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:12.560
Police Special Branch to begin monitoring&nbsp;
Simpson’s movements, the relationship between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:19.280
her and the heir to the throne had become a matter&nbsp;
of considerable concern to the royal family and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:25.520
the Conservative Party Prime Minister, Stanley&nbsp;
Baldwin, when he entered government that summer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:31.920
Although news of the affair had not become public&nbsp;
knowledge at that time, it was widely believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:31.920 --> 00:30:38.800
that if it did it would become a cause of major&nbsp;
scandal, both because Simpson was a divorcee at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:38.800 --> 00:30:45.600
a time when divorce still carried considerable&nbsp;
social stigma and also because Edward and she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:45.600 --> 00:30:52.640
were romantically involved while Wallis was still&nbsp;
married to her second husband. The affair would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.640 --> 00:31:00.880
soon change the course of Albert’s life. 
Albert’s father, King George V, died on the 20th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:09.600
of January 1936, in large part owing to a lung&nbsp;
condition exacerbated by lifelong chain smoking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:15.920
underlying medical conditions and habits which&nbsp;
were shared by his sons and which plagued their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:24.880
later lives as well. He had been considerably&nbsp;
ill since the mid-1920s, but by 1935 matters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:24.880 --> 00:31:32.480
were very poor indeed. In his final months he had&nbsp;
expressed his hopes that if Edward continued with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:38.560
his relationship with Simpson that they would&nbsp;
not have children and that the way would soon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.560 --> 00:31:46.080
be clear for Albert to succeed to the throne one&nbsp;
day. That would come sooner rather than later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:46.080 --> 00:31:52.080
Although Edward immediately ascended to the throne&nbsp;
as King Edward VIII following his father’s death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.080 --> 00:31:59.760
in January 1936, there were discussions taking&nbsp;
place immediately within Baldwin’s government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:06.400
about what course should be followed if Edward&nbsp;
insisted on marrying Simpson. As Edward did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:12.240
not have any children, Albert was necessarily&nbsp;
part of these discussions from their inception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.240 --> 00:32:20.080
as he was next in line to the throne. It was clear&nbsp;
that if Edward were forced to abdicate, Albert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:26.800
would almost certainly succeed him, although&nbsp;
there were rumours in the mid-to-late 1930s that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:33.120
the government was considering the possibility of&nbsp;
one of Albert’s two younger brothers, Prince Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:40.000
and Prince George, as possible candidates&nbsp;
to succeed Edward if the crisis deepened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:47.040
George, it was held at the time, was viewed in&nbsp;
particular as a possible king, as he and his wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:47.040 --> 00:32:52.800
Princess Marina of Greece and Denmark, had&nbsp;
become parents to a son, Prince Edward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.800 --> 00:33:00.960
in October 1935 and thus he would have a male&nbsp;
heir already if he became king. However, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.960 --> 00:33:07.600
is no evidence to suggest that the idea of Henry&nbsp;
or George succeeding Edward was ever seriously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:07.600 --> 00:33:13.760
entertained by Baldwin’s government and the plan&nbsp;
from the very start of the Abdication Crisis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:13.760 --> 00:33:20.960
was for Albert to succeed his brother if Edward&nbsp;
ended up renouncing his throne. 
	
&nbsp;

00:33:20.960 --> 00:33:28.160
Edward’s coronation was planned for the 12th of&nbsp;
May 1937. He would not remain as king for long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:35.440
enough for it to be held though. The first months&nbsp;
of his reign saw a growing standoff with Baldwin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:35.440 --> 00:33:42.080
concerning his relationship with Wallis Simpson.&nbsp;
Edward was seemingly determined to marry her and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.080 --> 00:33:49.840
for her part Wallis was taking steps to divorce&nbsp;
her second husband in advance of marrying Edward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:49.840 --> 00:33:56.240
She had informed friends that she expected to be&nbsp;
crowned as queen the day that Edward was crowned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.240 --> 00:34:04.320
as king. This would not be the case. Baldwin was&nbsp;
utterly opposed to Edward’s proposed marriage and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.320 --> 00:34:12.080
in the autumn of 1936 began liaising extensively&nbsp;
with the wider royal family, particularly Bertie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:18.160
who was reluctantly acclimatising himself to&nbsp;
the reality of succeeding his brother within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:18.160 --> 00:34:25.600
a matter of weeks, a development which he had&nbsp;
no desire to see occurring. News of the affair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:25.600 --> 00:34:31.760
eventually broke and it was made known to the&nbsp;
nation in the newspapers on the 2nd of December&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:31.760 --> 00:34:40.080
1936. Thereafter, despite efforts by some senior&nbsp;
members of parliament such as Winston Churchill to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.080 --> 00:34:47.360
support Edward’s right to marry whom he pleased,&nbsp;
it became abundantly clear that parliament sided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.360 --> 00:34:54.080
with Baldwin’s approach. Pressured into&nbsp;
making a swift decision, Edward agreed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.080 --> 00:35:00.800
abdicate rather than end his relationship with&nbsp;
Simpson. He did so on the 11th of December,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:00.800 --> 00:35:08.640
upon which Albert succeeded as King of Britain and&nbsp;
Emperor of India, taking the regnal name George VI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:16.480
in honour of his father. He was a reluctant king&nbsp;
and later revealed that when he had to visit his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:16.480 --> 00:35:23.600
mother and tell her the news of the abdication&nbsp;
and his assumption of the throne, he wept. 
 
&nbsp;

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:30.400
George rose to the position of king well.&nbsp;
His style of rule was modest and undramatic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:30.400 --> 00:35:36.960
in stark contrast to the controversy and drama&nbsp;
which had surrounded Edward as Prince of Wales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:44.400
and during his brief time as king. Over the&nbsp;
next fifteen or so years he would fulfil the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.400 --> 00:35:51.040
role of monarch and its constitutional remit&nbsp;
very well, rarely exceeding the role which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:56.400
the monarchy was largely confined to by the&nbsp;
middle of the twentieth century, which was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.400 --> 00:36:04.000
represent the royal establishment well and act&nbsp;
in a ceremonial capacity. Nevertheless, this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:04.000 --> 00:36:11.200
still an important function, particularly so when&nbsp;
Britain entered a period of extreme hardship from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.200 --> 00:36:19.680
the autumn of 1939 onwards. Moreover, George’s&nbsp;
modest and unassuming personality was a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:19.680 --> 00:36:25.520
foil to the larger than life character of&nbsp;
Winston Churchill as Prime Minister when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:25.520 --> 00:36:32.720
war would come just a few years into his reign.&nbsp;
Politically George was conservative in his views,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.720 --> 00:36:38.960
but not staunchly so and was well-suited&nbsp;
to overseeing the gradual modernisation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.960 --> 00:36:47.120
the country both socially and culturally.
George had come to power at a time when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.120 --> 00:36:54.880
political map of Europe was in flux. Following the&nbsp;
end of the First World War in 1918, the continent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:54.880 --> 00:37:02.560
had experienced five years of brutal revolutions&nbsp;
and civil wars in regions like Russia, Turkey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:12.240
Poland, Ireland and Germany. But eventually in&nbsp;
1923 and 1924 the chaos subsided and several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:18.640
years of major economic growth and prosperity&nbsp;
had followed. This was checked by the Wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.640 --> 00:37:26.640
Street Crash in the autumn of 1929 and the Great&nbsp;
Depression which followed. As renewed political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:34.400
turmoil arose across Europe many countries turned&nbsp;
to more extreme politics. In Central Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:40.960
in particular, far-right nationalist and fascist&nbsp;
parties had emerged to claim power in countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:49.600
like Austria, Hungary and above all Germany where&nbsp;
the Nazis led by Adolf Hitler seized power early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.600 --> 00:37:58.640
in 1933. Conversely, Eastern Europe was dominated&nbsp;
by the totalitarian Soviet Union led by Joseph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:58.640 --> 00:38:05.360
Stalin. Those few countries which retained a&nbsp;
democratic governmental system were threatened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:12.960
by the vying forces of fascism and communism and&nbsp;
shortly before George succeeded to the throne a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:12.960 --> 00:38:20.160
bitter civil war had broken out in Spain between&nbsp;
these left and right-wing political forces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:20.160 --> 00:38:26.400
The task before Britain in the first years of&nbsp;
George’s reign was to navigate this difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:32.720
political environment, preventing the rise of&nbsp;
both the British Union of Fascists under Oswald&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:40.640
Mosley and excessive social unrest wrought by&nbsp;
the political left. And George’s task in acting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:46.800
as head of state at this time was not helped&nbsp;
by Edward and Wallis’s decision to undertake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:46.800 --> 00:38:56.080
an unofficial tour of Nazi Germany in the autumn&nbsp;
of 1937, one in which Edward clearly displayed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.080 --> 00:39:03.600
appreciation of German National Socialism. 
When George became king, Britain was at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:10.400
crossroads in terms of how to approach the German&nbsp;
threat. It could begin rearming rapidly in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:16.480
to deter Germany from further aggression&nbsp;
or try to appease Hitler and the Nazis by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:22.320
granting them concessions, principally in the&nbsp;
shape of reversals of some of the more punitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:27.760
aspects of the Treaty of Versailles which&nbsp;
had brought the First World War to an end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:34.320
George was in many ways a favourer of appeasement,&nbsp;
but the principle architect of this approach was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:34.320 --> 00:39:41.600
Neville Chamberlain who succeeded Baldwin in&nbsp;
May 1937 when he stood down as Prime Minister.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.600 --> 00:39:48.720
Chamberlain continued a policy of slow rearmament,&nbsp;
while also allowing Germany to re-emerge as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:48.720 --> 00:39:55.600
major power in Central Europe. Thus, few&nbsp;
objections were raised when the Anschluss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:55.600 --> 00:40:03.600
the union of Germany and Austria into a Greater&nbsp;
Germany, was undertaken by the Nazis in March 1938&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:10.480
in direct violation of the peace treaties which&nbsp;
had brought the war to an end. George supported&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:10.480 --> 00:40:17.120
Chamberlain in this approach, but in doing so he&nbsp;
was actually following the constitutional remit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:17.120 --> 00:40:23.680
the monarchy by the 1930s, which was to support&nbsp;
the government of the day and its decisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:23.680 --> 00:40:31.440
regardless of whether or not those same policies&nbsp;
ran contrary to the monarch’s own views. 
&nbsp;

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:38.800
In one instance, and a particularly significant&nbsp;
one at that, George did directly associate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:44.000
himself with Chamberlain’s policy. Following&nbsp;
the annexation of Austria in the spring of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:50.400
1938 the Nazis had turned their attention to&nbsp;
the Sudetenland, the German-speaking region of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:57.360
western Czechoslovakia, making claims to this&nbsp;
territory. Eventually a diplomatic conference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:57.360 --> 00:41:04.800
was convened to be held in Munich in September&nbsp;
1938. In the lead up to it George offered to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:12.640
write directly to Hitler to try to appeal to him&nbsp;
as one ex-serviceman to another to try to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:12.640 --> 00:41:20.000
war. This was well-intended, though considerably&nbsp;
naïve in retrospect. When Chamberlain reached an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:26.160
agreement with Hitler at Munich to allow Germany&nbsp;
to annex the Sudetenland in return for a promise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.160 --> 00:41:32.000
of no further aggressive actions or claims on&nbsp;
its neighbours’ territory, George sent him a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:38.000
message requesting him to visit Buckingham Palace&nbsp;
immediately on his return to England so that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.000 --> 00:41:44.960
king could express his immense congratulations&nbsp;
on what he perceived to be a major diplomatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.960 --> 00:41:50.240
victory. The appearance of the monarch and the&nbsp;
Prime Minister on the balcony of Buckingham&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.240 --> 00:41:56.000
Palace together when Chamberlain arrived in&nbsp;
England was a striking statement about their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.000 --> 00:42:04.080
combined belief in the success of appeasement. But&nbsp;
they would soon realise how misguided their faith&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:04.080 --> 00:42:11.920
in the agreement reached at Munich was. 
In the summer of 1939, despite the troubled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:11.920 --> 00:42:17.200
political headwinds in Europe, George and&nbsp;
Elizabeth headed across the Atlantic Ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:17.200 --> 00:42:23.520
and visited the United States. The tour of the&nbsp;
US was undertaken on the invitation of Present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:23.520 --> 00:42:31.120
Franklin D. Roosevelt. Occurring between the 7th&nbsp;
and 12th of June, it has a significance as being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:37.680
the first time that a British monarch had ever&nbsp;
visited the country. No British monarch had agreed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.680 --> 00:42:44.480
to do so since the US, which had been born out of&nbsp;
Britain’s colonies in North America, had declared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.480 --> 00:42:52.160
its independence in 1776 and even prior to this&nbsp;
no monarch had visited the colonies since their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:52.160 --> 00:42:58.160
establishment in the early seventeenth century.&nbsp;
The tour took in much of the East Coast, with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:58.160 --> 00:43:04.800
visits to Washington D.C. and New York as well&nbsp;
as Mount Vernon, the home of George Washington in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:10.960
Virginia. The state visit was an important one in&nbsp;
making the British royals visible to the American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:18.240
public and was conceived of by Roosevelt as a way&nbsp;
of generating support in the US for providing aid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.240 --> 00:43:25.440
to Britain in the event of war breaking out.&nbsp;
It was a shrewd diplomatic move, one which did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:25.440 --> 00:43:31.360
not see US sentiment in favour of intervening in&nbsp;
the Second World War when it initially broke out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:31.360 --> 00:43:38.000
but which helped Roosevelt to persuade Congress&nbsp;
to provide financial and material support to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:45.280
Britain in the early stages of the war. 
Close ties between Britain and the US would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.280 --> 00:43:50.320
soon be needed, as Chamberlain’s efforts&nbsp;
at appeasement were proven to have been in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:50.320 --> 00:43:58.000
vain by the time George and Elizabeth toured&nbsp;
the US in the summer of 1939. No sooner had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:58.000 --> 00:44:04.640
the dust settled on the Munich Agreement and the&nbsp;
Sudetenland been annexed into a greater Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:04.640 --> 00:44:10.640
than Hitler and the other senior members of&nbsp;
the Nazi regime began turning their attentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:10.640 --> 00:44:18.480
towards further land grabs. The winter of 1938&nbsp;
was relatively calm, but the following March&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:26.000
the Munich accords were torn up as German troops&nbsp;
entered Czechoslovakia and occupied the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:33.600
which became a protectorate of Nazi Germany. Just&nbsp;
days later the city of Memel on the Baltic Sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:39.680
coast was annexed after being threatened with an&nbsp;
aerial bombardment by the German foreign minister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:39.680 --> 00:44:46.080
Joachim von Ribbentrop. By now Britain and&nbsp;
France had begun to accelerate the speed of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:52.480
their rearmament in preparation for the inevitable&nbsp;
conflict, but they were far behind where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:52.480 --> 00:44:59.520
needed to be. The Nazis were aware of this and&nbsp;
consequently accelerated their own march to war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:59.520 --> 00:45:06.800
In the summer of 1939 their attentions turned&nbsp;
to Poland, making diplomatic claims to Polish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:06.800 --> 00:45:13.840
territory which Germany had been forced to cede&nbsp;
in 1919 as part of the Treaty of Versailles which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:13.840 --> 00:45:22.800
brought the First World War to an end. Finally, in&nbsp;
late August 1939 a false flag operation was run to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:22.800 --> 00:45:30.560
make Poland seem like the aggressor in Eastern&nbsp;
Europe. On the 1st of September 1939 Germany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.560 --> 00:45:38.000
declared war on its eastern neighbour and invaded&nbsp;
Poland. Two days later, in response to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:38.000 --> 00:45:47.520
aggression, Britain and France went to war with&nbsp;
the Nazis. The Second World War had commenced. 
&nbsp;

00:45:47.520 --> 00:45:55.120
As the King of Britain and Emperor of India the&nbsp;
task fell to George on the 3rd of September 1939&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:55.120 --> 00:46:03.040
to address the nation upon Britain’s declaration&nbsp;
of war on Germany earlier that day. At 6pm that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:10.000
evening he delivered his speech, broadcast over&nbsp;
the radio. While Winston Churchill’s addresses to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:16.960
the nation during the war usually garner greater&nbsp;
attention, George’s on Britain’s entry into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:16.960 --> 00:46:25.680
war was also galvanising. In it he stated, “In&nbsp;
this grave hour, perhaps the most fateful in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:25.680 --> 00:46:34.560
history, I send to all my peoples, both at home&nbsp;
and over seas, this message with the same depth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:42.080
of feeling for each one of you as if I were able&nbsp;
to cross your threshold and speak to you myself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:42.080 --> 00:46:49.600
For the second time in the lives of most&nbsp;
of us we are at war. Over and over again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:49.600 --> 00:46:56.320
we have tried to find a peaceful way out of the&nbsp;
differences between ourselves and those who are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:56.320 --> 00:47:05.360
now our enemies. But it has been in vain…If&nbsp;
one and all be resolutely faithful today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:05.360 --> 00:47:14.560
ready for whatever service or sacrifice it may&nbsp;
demand, with God’s help we shall prevail.” 
&nbsp;

00:47:14.560 --> 00:47:19.040
George’s maiden speech to the nation&nbsp;
during the conflict was delivered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:19.040 --> 00:47:24.160
without any trace of the stutter which&nbsp;
had plagued him for much of his youth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:24.160 --> 00:47:30.880
Although the award-winning film The King’s Speech&nbsp;
contains many aspects of George’s story which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:30.880 --> 00:47:38.400
historically accurate, his challenges concerning&nbsp;
his stutter were primarily faced and overcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:46.480
with the assistance of Lionel Logue in the&nbsp;
mid-to-late 1920s, though George did periodically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:46.480 --> 00:47:52.800
consult with Logue over the years including&nbsp;
during the Second World War. Nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.800 --> 00:47:59.520
the film is inaccurate in suggesting that the king&nbsp;
only began to confront his stutter in the period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:59.520 --> 00:48:05.520
immediately before the war. 
	
With the onset of the war there was a growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:05.520 --> 00:48:11.200
problem in the heart of government. Neville&nbsp;
Chamberlain remained as Prime Minister and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:11.200 --> 00:48:16.320
retained the support of the bulk of the&nbsp;
Conservative Party. However, there was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:16.320 --> 00:48:22.400
rebellious faction amongst the Tories and many&nbsp;
in Britain felt that Chamberlain’s position was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:22.400 --> 00:48:28.480
untenable given that he had championed the&nbsp;
policy of appeasing Germany after he became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:28.480 --> 00:48:37.200
Prime Minister in 1937. Matters came to a head&nbsp;
in early May 1940 during the so-called Norway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:37.200 --> 00:48:43.520
Debate in the House of Commons, which began&nbsp;
concerning British efforts to open a front in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:43.520 --> 00:48:51.280
Northern Norway following the country’s occupation&nbsp;
by the Nazis, but which soon morphed into a wider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:51.280 --> 00:48:58.080
debate on Chamberlain’s management of the war.&nbsp;
It became clear that he could not remain on as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:58.080 --> 00:49:04.960
Prime Minister, but there was a debate as to who&nbsp;
should succeed him, with some favouring Winston&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:04.960 --> 00:49:12.080
Churchill, a long-standing Conservative critic of&nbsp;
the Nazis and appeasement and others supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:12.080 --> 00:49:19.040
the candidature of Lord Halifax, an ally of&nbsp;
Chamberlain’s who was not entirely opposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:19.040 --> 00:49:26.000
negotiating peace terms with Germany. George&nbsp;
was initially in support of Halifax, holding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:26.000 --> 00:49:32.720
a grudge against Churchill over his support for&nbsp;
Edward and opposition to George becoming king&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:32.720 --> 00:49:41.360
back in the early winter of 1936. However, as&nbsp;
events unfolded in the early summer of 1940 it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:48.800
became clear that Churchill was the candidate who&nbsp;
could command cross-party support in parliament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:48.800 --> 00:49:58.240
and on the 10th of May 1940 George asked Churchill&nbsp;
to form a new government. The case was urgent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:58.240 --> 00:50:04.640
as the Germans had invaded Belgium and the&nbsp;
Netherlands that morning heading towards France.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:04.640 --> 00:50:12.800
A cross-party coalition government conceived&nbsp;
on the widest basis was soon established. 
&nbsp;

00:50:12.800 --> 00:50:19.520
Though he opposed Churchill’s ascent as Prime&nbsp;
Minister initially, once he occupied 10 Downing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:19.520 --> 00:50:25.680
Street, the relationship between George and&nbsp;
Winston became one of the closest between any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:25.680 --> 00:50:31.840
British monarch and Prime Minister in modern&nbsp;
history. The exigencies of the war ensured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:31.840 --> 00:50:38.400
that they had to meet regularly and they soon&nbsp;
bonded over their common interest in the Navy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:38.400 --> 00:50:44.320
Churchill having served as First Lord of the&nbsp;
Admiralty during the First World War while George&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:44.320 --> 00:50:52.320
was at sea in the North Atlantic. Things grew from&nbsp;
there. By the late autumn of 1940 their formal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:58.960
meetings had been replaced by informal lunches&nbsp;
between king and prime minister every Tuesday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:58.960 --> 00:51:05.680
ones which would often last for several hours&nbsp;
and in which Churchill related the actions of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:11.200
government, while George explained what he&nbsp;
felt the mood of the nation was based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:11.200 --> 00:51:18.000
his extensive meetings with the public, which&nbsp;
were taking place on an almost daily basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:18.000 --> 00:51:23.280
We know of the considerable friendship which&nbsp;
developed between the pair in the course of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:23.280 --> 00:51:31.120
war owing to George having recorded them regularly&nbsp;
in his diary. It was not always smooth sailing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:31.120 --> 00:51:37.680
notably in the spring of 1944 when Churchill&nbsp;
had to convince the king that he could not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:37.680 --> 00:51:43.840
take part in the D-Day landings, not even&nbsp;
on board the warships at the rear once the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:43.840 --> 00:51:49.840
beachheads had been secured, but generally&nbsp;
the relationship was a successful one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:49.840 --> 00:51:57.040
in large part because Churchill encouraged&nbsp;
George, a naturally shy and retiring man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:57.040 --> 00:52:04.560
that he had a considerable public role to play in&nbsp;
the war. He made him feel useful. A sign of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:04.560 --> 00:52:11.040
affinity for one another would be seen many years&nbsp;
later, when Churchill was delivered the news of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:11.040 --> 00:52:17.680
George’s passing at 10 Downing Street, he was&nbsp;
said to have laid aside his papers and stated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:17.680 --> 00:52:26.160
“Bad news, the worst”, and descended into a deep&nbsp;
gloom for several days. 
	
&nbsp;

00:52:26.160 --> 00:52:32.560
George’s close relationship with Churchill was&nbsp;
in many ways forged in the dark days of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:32.560 --> 00:52:39.600
autumn of 1940. Following the Nazi invasion and&nbsp;
rapid conquest of the Low Countries and France&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:47.280
in the summer of 1940 the Blitz, a bombing&nbsp;
campaign of Britain initiated by the Nazis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:47.280 --> 00:52:53.920
combined with a naval blockade of Britain in&nbsp;
the North Atlantic, commenced. The Blitz began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:53.920 --> 00:53:00.560
on the 7th of September with the goal of bringing&nbsp;
Britain to negotiate peace terms without the Nazis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:00.560 --> 00:53:06.960
having to launch a land invasion of Britain.&nbsp;
London was the prime target from the beginning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:13.200
but George and Elizabeth took the decision&nbsp;
to remain in the capital. It was a hazardous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:13.200 --> 00:53:19.840
decision. Over 1,000 people alone were killed&nbsp;
in the city on the first night of the bombing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:19.840 --> 00:53:26.720
campaign and on the 13th of September the king&nbsp;
and queen were very nearly killed when several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:26.720 --> 00:53:33.680
bombs landed on Buckingham Palace. More broadly,&nbsp;
the royal family underwent the same rationing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:33.680 --> 00:53:40.480
was imposed on the entire British public during&nbsp;
the war years and the sense of shared struggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:40.480 --> 00:53:47.440
galvanised the nation and won George and Elizabeth&nbsp;
the admiration of the British people even as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:47.440 --> 00:53:57.280
Blitz dragged on for eight long months through to&nbsp;
May 1941. By the time it ended over 40,000 British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:57.280 --> 00:54:04.240
civilians were killed and two million homes&nbsp;
had been damaged or destroyed, the majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:04.240 --> 00:54:11.280
of the damage being inflicted on London. 
The worst of the Blitz and the naval blockade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:11.280 --> 00:54:19.120
ended in the spring of 1941. This was entirely&nbsp;
owing to the general drift of the conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:19.120 --> 00:54:26.240
Between the summer of 1940 after the swift fall&nbsp;
of France, Britain and the North Atlantic became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:26.240 --> 00:54:33.440
the crucible of the war. The king needed to be&nbsp;
visible during this, Britain’s darkest hour in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:33.440 --> 00:54:40.880
the conflict. However, from the summer of 1941&nbsp;
onwards the focus of matters shifted as Hitler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:40.880 --> 00:54:46.960
and the Nazis abandoned their designs on&nbsp;
forcing Britain to surrender and instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:46.960 --> 00:54:53.920
turned their attentions eastwards to the Soviet&nbsp;
Union, undertaking the largest land invasion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:53.920 --> 00:55:00.720
in military history. Thereafter the Eastern&nbsp;
Front became the focus of the war in Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:00.720 --> 00:55:07.360
while after the entry of the United States&nbsp;
into the conflict in December 1941 Britain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:07.360 --> 00:55:13.680
the US and the Commonwealth nations turned their&nbsp;
attentions to gaining victory in the North Africa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:13.680 --> 00:55:19.600
campaign against the Italians and the German&nbsp;
expeditionary force which had been dispatched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:19.600 --> 00:55:27.360
there. They finally emerged victorious in the&nbsp;
spring of 1943, after which a Southern Front was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:27.360 --> 00:55:35.280
opened in Italy by the Western Allies. Twelve&nbsp;
months later, in the summer of 1944, a Western&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:35.280 --> 00:55:42.640
Front was established with the D-Day landings and&nbsp;
the invasion of France. From that point onwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:42.640 --> 00:55:49.280
the course of the war and the result seemed&nbsp;
destined to be one of Allied victory.  
&nbsp;

00:55:49.920 --> 00:55:56.560
In September 1940, in the aftermath of the&nbsp;
evacuation of the British Expeditionary Force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:56.560 --> 00:56:02.160
from Dunkirk in northern France earlier that&nbsp;
summer, and the commencement of the Blitz and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:02.160 --> 00:56:08.080
the Battle of the North Atlantic, George&nbsp;
championed the creation of two new awards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:08.080 --> 00:56:14.960
which would be bestowed by the crown. The George&nbsp;
Cross and the George Medal were both created in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:14.960 --> 00:56:21.440
September 1940. Unlike the Victoria Cross, which&nbsp;
had been established during the long reign of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:21.440 --> 00:56:28.400
George’s great-grandmother, and other military&nbsp;
honours, the George Cross and George Medal were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:28.400 --> 00:56:34.880
to be awarded to anyone who was deemed to have&nbsp;
conducted themselves with gallantry and bravery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:34.880 --> 00:56:41.760
be they civilians or soldiers. In the context&nbsp;
of the Blitz, when ordinary Londoners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:41.760 --> 00:56:48.320
and in particular fire-fighters and police, were&nbsp;
effectively the front line soldiers in the war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:48.320 --> 00:56:55.360
against Germany, such awards were deemed necessary&nbsp;
by the king. The George Cross would become the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:55.360 --> 00:57:02.400
civilian equivalent of the Victoria Cross, the&nbsp;
highest military award of its kind. In announcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:02.400 --> 00:57:10.320
the creation of the new honour, the king stated&nbsp;
that, quote, “I have decided to create, at once,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:10.320 --> 00:57:17.440
a new mark of honour for men and women in all&nbsp;
walks of civilian life. I propose to give my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:17.440 --> 00:57:24.320
name to this new distinction, which will consist&nbsp;
of the George Cross, which will rank next to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:24.320 --> 00:57:32.080
Victoria Cross, and the George Medal for wider&nbsp;
distribution.” It was to be awarded for “acts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:32.080 --> 00:57:40.080
the greatest heroism or of the most conspicuous&nbsp;
courage in circumstances of extreme danger.” 
&nbsp;

00:57:40.080 --> 00:57:45.280
Over the course of the war George would&nbsp;
personally present the awards to dozens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:45.280 --> 00:57:51.760
of soldiers and civilians. Those who were&nbsp;
honoured included the likes of Stuart Archer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:51.760 --> 00:57:59.040
a bomb-diffusing expert who had diffused over 200&nbsp;
bombs that had landed undetonated in England by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:59.040 --> 00:58:07.040
September 1941. John Bridge was another medal of&nbsp;
the Cross for his role in defusing dozens of bombs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:07.040 --> 00:58:13.600
which landed in urban centres across England.&nbsp;
The George Medal was granted in similar cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:13.600 --> 00:58:20.160
often to members of the Commonwealth nations. For&nbsp;
instance, Margaret Irene Anderson, an Australian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:20.160 --> 00:58:26.320
staff nurse on board the Empire Star, was awarded&nbsp;
the Medal for her gallantry during the evacuation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:26.320 --> 00:58:34.720
of Singapore in the face of the Japanese onslaught&nbsp;
in 1942. Back home, Charity Bick was awarded the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:34.720 --> 00:58:41.920
George Medal by the king. She had lied about her&nbsp;
age at just 14 in order to be accepted into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:41.920 --> 00:58:48.640
Air Raid Precautions unit in 1939. During an&nbsp;
air raid on West Bromwich by the Germans the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:48.640 --> 00:58:55.120
following August she delivered messages on her&nbsp;
bicycle to a nearby RAF control room and helped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:55.120 --> 00:59:01.040
her father put out an incendiary bomb that fell&nbsp;
on the roof of a shop. In awarding these honours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:01.040 --> 00:59:08.320
to individuals like Archer, Bridge, Anderson&nbsp;
and Bick, George galvanised public sentiment to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:08.320 --> 00:59:17.280
continue the struggle against Germany during the&nbsp;
dark days of late 1940 and early 1941 when Britain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:17.280 --> 00:59:25.360
stood largely alone against the Nazi threat.  
George and Elizabeth contributed to the war cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:25.360 --> 00:59:32.400
in other ways. From 1940 onwards the king and the&nbsp;
queen consort were regular visitors to hospitals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:32.400 --> 00:59:39.760
and various fronts in England and further afield.&nbsp;
From the summer of 1940 onwards they regularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:39.760 --> 00:59:45.920
visited sites of extensive bombing raids to&nbsp;
console the victims’ relatives and to meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:45.920 --> 00:59:52.800
the wounded. Often these duties were divided&nbsp;
up, with George heading for military bases and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:52.800 --> 00:59:59.760
Elizabeth touring London’s hospitals and those in&nbsp;
the other major cities. One might look at these as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:59.760 --> 01:00:06.800
merely symbolic gestures, but symbolic gestures&nbsp;
at a time of civilian endurance were what was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:06.800 --> 01:00:13.120
needed at the time and the king and queen earned&nbsp;
plaudits for their very visible public presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:13.120 --> 01:00:19.680
throughout the Blitz and the remainder of the&nbsp;
war. As the focus of the conflict shifted away&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:19.680 --> 01:00:27.200
from Britain in 1941 and the Western Allies&nbsp;
began taking the offensive on several fronts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:27.200 --> 01:00:33.520
George often left England, heading to the&nbsp;
front lines in North Africa and the island&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:33.520 --> 01:00:42.160
fortress of Malta in 1943 and visiting France,&nbsp;
the Low Countries and Italy in 1944 after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:42.160 --> 01:00:50.720
Southern and Western Fronts had been opened. 
By 1944 the war was entering its final stages as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:50.720 --> 01:00:56.400
Germany found itself being advanced on from&nbsp;
the east by the Soviets and from the south&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:56.400 --> 01:01:03.680
and west by the Western Allies. George did not&nbsp;
play an entirely silent role in these affairs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:03.680 --> 01:01:10.160
He made some contributions towards Allied&nbsp;
strategy, notably in 1943 when he proposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:10.160 --> 01:01:17.040
that the Allies should forego opening a new front&nbsp;
in France in favour of pushing resources into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:23.440
Southern Front in Italy, a strategy which&nbsp;
Churchill was considerably in favour of and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:23.440 --> 01:01:28.960
sent along to the military chiefs of staff.&nbsp;
In the end, though, George saw the logic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:28.960 --> 01:01:34.480
opening a front in northern France and on the&nbsp;
evening of the D-Day landings he delivered a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:34.480 --> 01:01:41.440
rousing broadcast in which he recalled the grim&nbsp;
position Britain had been in four years earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:41.440 --> 01:01:50.000
before stating that, quote, “once more a supreme&nbsp;
test has to be faced. This time, the challenge is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:50.000 --> 01:01:58.480
not to fight to survive but to fight to win the&nbsp;
final victory for the good cause.” That eventual&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:58.480 --> 01:02:05.440
victory would take another eleven months to&nbsp;
secure, but in the end as Soviet troops closed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:05.440 --> 01:02:13.040
central Berlin and British, American, Canadian and&nbsp;
other Allied soldiers fanned out across Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:13.040 --> 01:02:22.480
Hitler killed himself and the Nazis surrendered&nbsp;
on the 8th of May 1945. That VE or Victory in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:22.480 --> 01:02:28.720
Europe Day, George and the rest of the royal&nbsp;
family appeared on the balconies of Buckingham&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:28.720 --> 01:02:36.960
Palace to celebrate with the British public the&nbsp;
end of the near six year long struggle. 
	
&nbsp;

01:02:36.960 --> 01:02:44.160
With victory in the war George’s role shifted from&nbsp;
being Britain’s war leader to overseeing the rapid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:44.160 --> 01:02:50.560
dismantling of its empire. Promises had been&nbsp;
made during the war to many interested parties&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:50.560 --> 01:02:56.640
concerning increased autonomy as the reward for&nbsp;
helping Britain in its struggle against Nazi&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:56.640 --> 01:03:04.640
Germany and the Empire of Japan. In particular,&nbsp;
the Cripps Mission of 1942 to India had promised&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:04.640 --> 01:03:10.800
the Indian National Congress leaders such as&nbsp;
Mahatma Gandhi that India would be allowed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:10.800 --> 01:03:17.520
hold elections and have greater self-determination&nbsp;
in the aftermath of the conflict if it committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:17.520 --> 01:03:27.120
fully to aiding Britain in its hour of need. Now&nbsp;
the debt fell due. In 1947 India was granted its&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:27.120 --> 01:03:34.000
independence and the British Raj was divided&nbsp;
up, so that the Muslim-majority areas in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:34.000 --> 01:03:41.680
north-west and north-east became the new state of&nbsp;
Pakistan, though the province of East Bengal would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:41.680 --> 01:03:49.840
later become the independent nation of Bangladesh.&nbsp;
George briefly remained as Emperor of India even&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:49.840 --> 01:03:58.480
after independence, but the title was abolished&nbsp;
entirely in 1948, though India and Pakistan would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:58.480 --> 01:04:05.440
remain as members of the British Commonwealth.&nbsp;
Thus, in the second half of the 1940s, George&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:05.440 --> 01:04:12.720
was overseeing the first steps of the post-war&nbsp;
transition from the Empire to Commonwealth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:12.720 --> 01:04:19.440
including the 1949 London Declaration which&nbsp;
was pursuant from India’s declaration of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:19.440 --> 01:04:26.640
itself as a republic and the removal of George&nbsp;
as head of state of that Commonwealth nation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:26.640 --> 01:04:33.760
George was cautiously in favour of this move,&nbsp;
provided India remained a Commonwealth nation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:33.760 --> 01:04:40.880
though the episode did see the Republic of&nbsp;
Ireland leave the Commonwealth entirely.   
	
&nbsp;

01:04:40.880 --> 01:04:48.960
The further dismantling of Britain’s empire would&nbsp;
gather pace in the 1950s, particularly from 1957&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:48.960 --> 01:04:56.640
onwards when the first wave of decolonisation&nbsp;
spread across Africa. By the mid-1960s Britain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:56.640 --> 01:05:02.480
would relinquish much of its control of its&nbsp;
territories in regions like Kenya, Uganda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:02.480 --> 01:05:09.280
Nigeria and Rhodesia, many of which new nations&nbsp;
in turn became members of the Commonwealth. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:09.280 --> 01:05:17.200
George would not live to see this. His health was&nbsp;
deteriorating already in the late 1940s, though he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:17.200 --> 01:05:24.640
was only just after entering his fifties. Like his&nbsp;
father before him, his lifelong chain smoking had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:24.640 --> 01:05:32.800
taken its toll on his health, as had the stresses&nbsp;
of the war years. Moreover, by the late 1940s he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:32.800 --> 01:05:38.880
was suffering from several circulatory problems&nbsp;
including Buerger’s Disease, which leads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:38.880 --> 01:05:47.680
clotting of small and medium arteries and which&nbsp;
is also exacerbated by smoking. By 1949 matters&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:47.680 --> 01:05:53.840
were serious and a planned tour of some of the&nbsp;
Commonwealth nations had to be cancelled, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:53.840 --> 01:06:00.880
for a time it was feared that George would have&nbsp;
to have one of his legs amputated. Unsurprisingly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:00.880 --> 01:06:07.360
by this time his eldest daughter and the heir&nbsp;
presumptive to the throne, Elizabeth, who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:07.360 --> 01:06:15.840
only 23 years of age, was carrying out more and&nbsp;
more royal duties by the end of the decade. 
&nbsp;

01:06:15.840 --> 01:06:24.720
Matters did not improve into the 1950s. In 1951&nbsp;
George had to have his left lung surgically&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:24.720 --> 01:06:30.880
removed after he developed lung cancer. He was&nbsp;
limited in his physical movements from that point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:30.880 --> 01:06:37.520
onwards, although the king attempted to remain&nbsp;
active, insisting on accompanying his daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:37.520 --> 01:06:45.040
and her husband, Prince Philip, to London Airport&nbsp;
on the 31st of January 1952 when they left for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:45.040 --> 01:06:52.160
tour of much of the empire. It was the last time&nbsp;
he would see his daughter and heir. George died&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:52.160 --> 01:07:00.320
in his sleep a week later on the 6th of February&nbsp;
1952 from a coronary thrombosis at Sandringham&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:00.320 --> 01:07:08.960
where he was born. He was just 56 years of age.&nbsp;
Owing to his premature death, Elizabeth succeeded&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:08.960 --> 01:07:18.160
to the throne of Britain at just 25 years of age&nbsp;
and as she lived to be 96 years herself her reign&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:18.160 --> 01:07:25.280
would be the longest in British history. News of&nbsp;
George’s death was released immediately and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:25.280 --> 01:07:31.120
mechanisms for the holding of a state funeral&nbsp;
were put in place. His body lay in state at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:31.120 --> 01:07:36.560
Westminster from the 11th of February onwards so&nbsp;
that the British public could pay their respects&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:36.560 --> 01:07:44.800
to the wartime king. His funeral was held on the&nbsp;
15th like those of so many British monarchs at St&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:44.800 --> 01:07:51.440
George’s Chapel at Windsor Castle. Afterwards&nbsp;
his remains were interred in the royal vault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:51.440 --> 01:08:00.160
though they only remained here until 1969, at&nbsp;
which time George was reinterred in the George VI&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:00.160 --> 01:08:07.440
Memorial Chapel. His remains lie there today with&nbsp;
those of his wife, Elizabeth, the Queen Mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:07.440 --> 01:08:14.880
who lived until 2002, outliving her husband&nbsp;
by half a century, and those of his daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:14.880 --> 01:08:22.800
the recently deceased Queen Elizabeth II and her&nbsp;
husband the Duke of Edinburgh, Prince Philip. 
&nbsp;

01:08:22.800 --> 01:08:29.120
George VI led Britain through one of the&nbsp;
most consequential periods in world history&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:29.120 --> 01:08:37.840
and arguably the most significant in Britain’s&nbsp;
long imperial story. For much of 1940 and 1941&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:37.840 --> 01:08:45.600
the country was the only major power standing&nbsp;
against Nazi Germany and the fascist threat. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:45.600 --> 01:08:52.640
that dark moment the country needed leadership.&nbsp;
It is generally understood to have come from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:52.640 --> 01:08:58.800
Winston Churchill, but there was also George&nbsp;
and Elizabeth as his queen consort who acted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:58.800 --> 01:09:05.360
as figureheads in the struggle against the Blitz&nbsp;
and the blockade of Britain by Germany. He rose&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:05.360 --> 01:09:13.200
extremely well to that occasion. Moreover, it came&nbsp;
from a man who was never supposed to become king,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:13.200 --> 01:09:20.240
his older brother’s love life and to a certain&nbsp;
extent his difficult personality having combined&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:20.240 --> 01:09:27.280
to ensure that his reign was a short one and&nbsp;
Edward had to abdicate in favour of George in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:27.280 --> 01:09:34.800
December 1936. When he did become King of Britain,&nbsp;
George cannot be said to have been a philosopher&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:34.800 --> 01:09:41.680
king or a particularly forceful personality, but&nbsp;
he offered a steady hand and humility at the helm&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:41.680 --> 01:09:48.800
of state which was fitting for the time period&nbsp;
in which he became monarch. Overcoming his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:48.800 --> 01:09:54.720
personal limitations, he won the respect&nbsp;
of the British people throughout the war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:54.720 --> 01:10:01.840
developed a close relationship with Churchill and&nbsp;
managed the transition from empire to commonwealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:01.840 --> 01:10:09.200
well in the aftermath of the conflict. Tragically,&nbsp;
his physical decline ensured that his reign was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:09.200 --> 01:10:16.320
cut short and that his last years were spent in&nbsp;
considerable pain. He should be remembered as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:16.320 --> 01:10:23.760
modest and humble, but effective king.  
What do you think of King George VI? Was it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:23.760 --> 01:10:30.400
a good thing that he became King of England and&nbsp;
that Edward abdicated the throne in 1936? Please&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.400 --> 01:10:40.000
let us know in the comment section, and in the&nbsp;
meantime, thank you very much for watching.
&nbsp;

01:10:46.240 --> 01:10:54.640
George VI and Elizabeth Bowes-Lyon were born less&nbsp;
than five years apart. He came into the world in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:54.640 --> 01:11:04.800
December 1895. She was born on the 4th of August&nbsp;
1900. However, despite being born relatively&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:04.800 --> 01:11:12.560
close to one another, they died half a century&nbsp;
apart. George’s chronic respiratory problems and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:12.560 --> 01:11:22.640
subsequent cancer led to his premature death in&nbsp;
February 1952 at 56 years of age. Elizabeth would&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:22.640 --> 01:11:33.600
live for a little over 50 years longer, dying in&nbsp;
March 2002 at 101 years of age. In that time, she&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:33.600 --> 01:11:40.320
became known as the Queen Mother, the matriarch&nbsp;
of the royal family who lived to see her children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:40.320 --> 01:11:47.440
grandchildren and many of her great-grandchildren&nbsp;
grow up. There is no sign that she ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:47.440 --> 01:11:54.960
contemplated remarrying or another relationship.&nbsp;
Instead, she continued the work she had carried&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:54.960 --> 01:12:02.640
out while Bertie was alive. She remained a rock&nbsp;
of stability and offered advice to her daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:02.640 --> 01:12:08.960
the new monarch, Queen Elizabeth II, where&nbsp;
previously she had been the pillar that her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:08.960 --> 01:12:16.640
husband relied on in the dark days of the war. No&nbsp;
other royal would epitomise the modernisation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:16.640 --> 01:12:24.080
the monarchy as much as the Queen Mother did. When&nbsp;
she was born, Queen Victoria sat on the throne&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:24.080 --> 01:12:32.400
and Britain’s empire stretched around the world.&nbsp;
When she died, the third millennium had started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:32.400 --> 01:12:44.640
Britain’s empire was long gone and the monarchy&nbsp;
as an institution had been transformed.
&nbsp;

01:12:50.320 --> 01:12:56.960
The woman known to history as ‘Queen Elizabeth,&nbsp;
the Queen Mother’ was born the Honourable&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:56.960 --> 01:13:06.160
Elizabeth Angela Marguerite Bowes-Lyon on the&nbsp;
4th of August 1900 at St Paul’s Walden Bury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:06.160 --> 01:13:12.640
a modest stately home near Hitchen&nbsp;
in Hertfordshire, England.  
 
&nbsp;

01:13:12.640 --> 01:13:20.160
One of ten children altogether, Elizabeth was the&nbsp;
fourth and youngest daughter of Claude Bowes-Lyon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:20.160 --> 01:13:26.960
the Lord Glamis who became the 14th Earl of&nbsp;
Strathmore and Kinghorne. When her father&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:26.960 --> 01:13:35.840
inherited the Scottish Earldom in 1904, she became&nbsp;
Lady Elizabeth Bowes-Lyon. The Bowes-Lyon family&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:35.840 --> 01:13:42.480
is descended from the Royal House of Scotland,&nbsp;
with one of Elizabeth’s 14th-century ancestors&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:42.480 --> 01:13:49.120
becoming the Thane of Glamis, whose family&nbsp;
seat was Glamis Castle the setting of William&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:49.120 --> 01:13:57.760
Shakespeare’s Macbeth some 300 years earlier.&nbsp;
Her mother was Lady Cecilia Cavendish-Bentinck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:57.760 --> 01:14:03.760
a descendent of William Cavendish-Bentinck,&nbsp;
the 3rd Duke of Portland and a former Prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:03.760 --> 01:14:13.920
Minister of Great Britain from 1783, and then&nbsp;
of the United Kingdom between 1807 and 1809.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:13.920 --> 01:14:19.440
Spending most of her childhood between&nbsp;
Glamis Castle and St Paul’s Walden Bury,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:19.440 --> 01:14:27.920
she engaged in field sports and enjoyed the&nbsp;
company of dogs and horses. Of all her siblings&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:27.920 --> 01:14:34.560
she was particularly close to her young brother&nbsp;
David. There was only a two-year age difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:34.560 --> 01:14:41.760
between them, and they were often inseparable&nbsp;
and liked to play pranks on people together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:41.760 --> 01:14:49.120
Elizabeth was also extremely well educated due&nbsp;
to her private tutoring at home, and by the age&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:49.120 --> 01:14:57.680
of 10, spoke French fluently. For a time she&nbsp;
was educated by a German Jewish governess Käthe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:57.680 --> 01:15:05.680
Kübler and passed the Oxford Local Examination&nbsp;
with distinction at the age of thirteen.
 
&nbsp;

01:15:06.880 --> 01:15:14.320
The Great Britain in which Elizabeth grew up&nbsp;
would have appeared to her peaceful and tranquil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:14.320 --> 01:15:20.480
and Elizabeth’s status as a Lady and as a&nbsp;
member of the upper classes meant that she had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:20.480 --> 01:15:28.160
a relatively easy life. Through the Victorian era,&nbsp;
Great Britain had become one of the most advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:28.160 --> 01:15:35.200
countries in the world thanks to its pre-eminence&nbsp;
in trade and an empire that reached all four&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:35.200 --> 01:15:45.600
corners of the globe. However, it was not without&nbsp;
momentous social and political change. 
 
&nbsp;

01:15:45.600 --> 01:15:54.960
Between 1910 and 1914, ‘The Great Unrest’ as&nbsp;
it was termed, saw a mass upsurge in labour&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:54.960 --> 01:16:03.280
militancy and wave upon wave of strikes. Four&nbsp;
thousand strikes were recorded involving around&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:03.280 --> 01:16:11.040
four million workers over that period and trade&nbsp;
union membership rose from 2 million to just over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:11.040 --> 01:16:20.240
4 million. Some actions were aggressive, violent&nbsp;
and insurgent. This was seen during the bitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:20.240 --> 01:16:29.680
South Wales coal strike in Tonypandy in 1910;&nbsp;
the railway and dock strikes of 1911; and in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:29.680 --> 01:16:36.000
general strike of transport workers in Liverpool&nbsp;
in the same year which resulted in clashes between&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:36.000 --> 01:16:45.200
strikers and troops. Triggered by anger over poor&nbsp;
wages, a 70-hour week and compulsory overtime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:45.200 --> 01:16:51.120
mass picketing at Llanelli railway station, on&nbsp;
the critical route from London to Ireland via&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:51.120 --> 01:16:59.920
the ferry at Fishguard, was brutally suppressed by&nbsp;
the police and troops. Two men were shot dead and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:59.920 --> 01:17:08.880
many others were wounded. The troops’ action led&nbsp;
to a day of widespread disorder and rioting. 
 
&nbsp;

01:17:08.880 --> 01:17:11.680
 
A movement to gain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:11.680 --> 01:17:21.280
women’s right to vote eventually succeeded through&nbsp;
acts of parliament in 1918 and 1928. It had become&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:21.280 --> 01:17:27.920
a national movement during the Victorian era with&nbsp;
the formation of the National Society for Women’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:27.920 --> 01:17:36.160
Suffrage founded in 1872. It remained largely&nbsp;
ineffective even after the creation of the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:36.160 --> 01:17:43.280
vocal National Union of Women’s Suffrage Societies&nbsp;
led by Millicent Fawcett which still maintained&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:43.280 --> 01:17:50.720
it could achieve its aims through peaceful means.&nbsp;
The campaign took on a more militant face with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:50.720 --> 01:17:57.600
establishment of the Women’s Social and Political&nbsp;
Union, led by Emmeline Pankhurst and her daughters&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:57.600 --> 01:18:06.080
Christabel and Sylvia. These were the suffragettes&nbsp;
whose tactics included parades, hunger strikes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:06.080 --> 01:18:14.720
stone throwing, and smashing windows. In 1913,&nbsp;
Suffragette Emily Davison became a martyr to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:14.720 --> 01:18:21.600
cause by throwing herself under the King’s horse&nbsp;
at the Epsom Derby that year. While politics was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:21.600 --> 01:18:29.600
put on hold at the start of the First World War,&nbsp;
limited suffrage was however extended in 1918&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:29.600 --> 01:18:39.520
not only to 8.4 million women but also to&nbsp;
5.6 million disenfranchised men. 1928 saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:39.520 --> 01:18:47.840
legislation to extend suffrage to all men&nbsp;
and women over the age of 21.
     
 
&nbsp;

01:18:47.840 --> 01:18:52.720
Another crucial moment which would come to&nbsp;
dominate British history and the life of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:52.720 --> 01:18:58.880
Elzabeth Bowes-Lyon in the 20th century&nbsp;
was the political situation in Ireland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:59.520 --> 01:19:05.920
A movement aimed at gaining self-government for&nbsp;
the island of Ireland had become the dominant&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:05.920 --> 01:19:13.680
movement in Irish nationalism since the 1870s.&nbsp;
Successive Home Rule Bills championed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:13.680 --> 01:19:19.280
Liberals had been defeated towards the end&nbsp;
of the 19th century either in the House of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:19.280 --> 01:19:28.560
Commons or the House of Lords. A third attempt in&nbsp;
1912 was seen as only effecting moderate reforms&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:28.560 --> 01:19:36.960
and inflamed militant nationalists eventually&nbsp;
leading to the Easter Rising in 1916, the Irish&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:36.960 --> 01:19:45.600
War of Independence and a treaty creating the&nbsp;
Irish Free State with Dominion status in 1922,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:45.600 --> 01:19:52.000
which allowed for the six-counties of the&nbsp;
north to remain part of the United Kingdom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:52.000 --> 01:20:02.320
The Irish Republic came into being in 1937.  
While these three issues dominated Elizabeth’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:02.320 --> 01:20:10.000
early years, they would eventually be eclipsed by&nbsp;
the outbreak of the First World War on the 4th of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:10.000 --> 01:20:19.120
August 1914, her 14th birthday. During the war,&nbsp;
her home of Glamis Castle became a convalescent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:19.120 --> 01:20:25.520
hospital for troops who had been injured in the&nbsp;
conflict, but who were expected to make a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:25.520 --> 01:20:32.480
recovery, and so did not need as much medical&nbsp;
attention as other patients. Elizabeth was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:32.480 --> 01:20:39.680
too young to become a nurse, but she did assist&nbsp;
with welfare work, tending to wounded soldiers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:39.680 --> 01:20:47.920
and helping with their rehabilitation, and by all&nbsp;
accounts was very popular and well-liked. Four of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:47.920 --> 01:20:54.480
her older brothers served during the First World&nbsp;
War. Her brother Fergus was an officer in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:54.480 --> 01:21:03.360
Scottish Black Watch Regiment but was wounded and&nbsp;
killed in action during the 1915 Battle of Loos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:03.360 --> 01:21:12.560
Her brother Michael was also a soldier, and on the&nbsp;
28th of April 1917 was reported missing in action.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:12.560 --> 01:21:19.520
It was later discovered that he had been wounded,&nbsp;
taken captive, and moved to a prisoner-of-war&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:19.520 --> 01:21:26.960
camp, where he stayed until the end of the&nbsp;
conflict. Strangely, one of the soldiers Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:26.960 --> 01:21:35.680
cared for, wrote in her autograph book that&nbsp;
she was to be ‘Hung, Drawn and Quartered … Hung&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:35.680 --> 01:21:42.960
in diamonds, drawn in a coach and four, and&nbsp;
quartered in the best house in the land.’
 
&nbsp;

01:21:42.960 --> 01:21:47.040
 
By tradition, young women from noble and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:47.040 --> 01:21:53.680
gentry families would be presented at court at the&nbsp;
start of the social season. After being presented&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:53.680 --> 01:21:59.600
to the British sovereign, these debutants, as&nbsp;
they were called, were allowed to take part in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:59.600 --> 01:22:06.960
all manner of balls, parties, and horse-racing&nbsp;
events which was a sign that they were eligible&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:06.960 --> 01:22:15.520
for marriage. Elizabeth was presented to&nbsp;
King George V in 1919. She was, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:15.520 --> 01:22:22.000
no stranger to the royal family with the children&nbsp;
of the then King George and Queen Mary occasional&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:22.000 --> 01:22:30.880
visitors at her home at Glamis Castle in Scotland.&nbsp;
In 1922, she acted as one of the bridesmaids at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:30.880 --> 01:22:38.720
the wedding of their only daughter, Princess Mary&nbsp;
to the 6th Earl of Harewood, Henry Lascelles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:38.720 --> 01:22:47.120
During one such high society event, Elizabeth was&nbsp;
introduced to Princess Mary’s brother, Albert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:47.120 --> 01:22:55.200
known as ‘Bertie’ within the family and who at&nbsp;
that time was second in line to the throne. Albert&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:55.200 --> 01:23:04.080
first proposed to her in 1921 when she was just&nbsp;
twenty years old, but Elizabeth turned him down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:04.080 --> 01:23:10.880
fearing the restrictions that a royal marriage&nbsp;
might bring to her life. Elizabeth did eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:10.880 --> 01:23:19.280
accept his proposal and on the 13th of January&nbsp;
1923, their engagement was publicly announced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:19.280 --> 01:23:25.520
Albert's choice of Elizabeth was seen&nbsp;
at the time as the monarchy modernising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:25.520 --> 01:23:33.440
as previously it was common for princes to&nbsp;
marry members of other royal houses.   
 
&nbsp;

01:23:33.440 --> 01:23:42.160
Prince Albert and Lady Elizabeth were wed on the&nbsp;
26th of April 1923 in Westminster Abbey, the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:42.160 --> 01:23:50.880
Royal Wedding to be held there since that of Anne&nbsp;
of Bohemia to Richard II in 1382. Breaking with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:50.880 --> 01:23:57.920
tradition, Elizabeth placed her wedding flowers on&nbsp;
top of the Tomb of the Unknown Warrior to honour&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.920 --> 01:24:06.000
her brother Fergus. Lady Elizabeth henceforth&nbsp;
became Her Royal Highness the Duchess of York.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:06.000 --> 01:24:13.600
After their wedding, she and Prince Albert quickly&nbsp;
began to perform royal engagements together. Their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:13.600 --> 01:24:20.160
first joint appearance after their wedding was&nbsp;
at the RAF procession in Hendon held on the 30th&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:20.160 --> 01:24:28.960
of June 1923. One of the most significant royal&nbsp;
engagements they had together however was in July&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:28.960 --> 01:24:36.960
2024, when they visited Northern Ireland. Due&nbsp;
to the success of this visit, the then Labour&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:36.960 --> 01:24:44.640
government decided that the couple should embark&nbsp;
on a tour of East Africa from December 1924 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:44.640 --> 01:24:53.360
April 1925. However, in November 1924, the&nbsp;
Conservatives defeated Labour in the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:53.360 --> 01:25:02.240
election, against a backdrop of political unrest&nbsp;
in what was then known as Anglo-Egyptian Sudan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:02.240 --> 01:25:08.400
modern Sudan and South Sudan, which saw the&nbsp;
assassination of the Governor-General Sir Lee&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:08.400 --> 01:25:17.040
Stack. Despite this, Albert and Elizabeth decided&nbsp;
to embark on their royal tour, visiting Aden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:17.040 --> 01:25:28.800
Uganda, Anglo-Egyptian Sudan, and Kenya, though&nbsp;
they avoided Egypt due to political tensions.
 
&nbsp;

01:25:28.800 --> 01:25:37.120
Around this time Elizabeth encouraged her husband&nbsp;
to see a speech therapist. All his life Albert&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:37.120 --> 01:25:44.400
had dreaded making speeches because of a severe&nbsp;
stammer. His closing speech at the British Empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:44.400 --> 01:25:52.480
Exhibition in 1925 had proved a particular ordeal.&nbsp;
The couple were introduced to an Australian speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:52.480 --> 01:26:00.960
therapist called Lionel Logue who suggested daily&nbsp;
hour-long vocal exercises which greatly reduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:00.960 --> 01:26:07.760
his stammer and gave him greater confidence&nbsp;
in public speaking. Whenever Albert was due&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:07.760 --> 01:26:15.200
to make a speech, Logue was often called in, and&nbsp;
he was regularly invited to the royal family's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:15.200 --> 01:26:22.880
Christmas dinner to oversee the King’s Christmas&nbsp;
message. The relationship was later dramatized in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:22.880 --> 01:26:31.840
the 2010 film The King’s Speech, directed by Tom&nbsp;
Hooper and which starred Geoffrey Rush as Logue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:31.840 --> 01:26:38.800
Colin Firth as King George VI and Helena&nbsp;
Bonham Carter in the role of Elizabeth.
&nbsp;

01:26:38.800 --> 01:26:43.760
 
 
On the 21st of April 1926,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:43.760 --> 01:26:52.160
their first child, Princess Elizabeth Alexandra&nbsp;
Mary, later to become Queen Elizabeth II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:52.160 --> 01:26:59.520
was born. Shortly after their daughter's birth,&nbsp;
Albert and Elizabeth embarked on another royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:59.520 --> 01:27:06.400
tour leaving their newborn daughter behind.&nbsp;
It’s known that Elizabeth greatly struggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:06.400 --> 01:27:13.840
with leaving their daughter behind, but their&nbsp;
tour to Australia. Jamaca, Fiji, and back via&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:13.840 --> 01:27:22.800
the Panama Canal was regarded at home as a great&nbsp;
success for public relations. Four years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:22.800 --> 01:27:32.480
their second daughter, Princess Margaret Rose, was&nbsp;
born on the 21st of August 1930. Margaret was born&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:32.480 --> 01:27:41.280
in Glamis Castle and was the first royal baby to&nbsp;
be born in Scotland since 1600. The registration&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:41.280 --> 01:27:48.480
of her birth was delayed, to avoid Margaret&nbsp;
being the 13th name in the parish register,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:48.480 --> 01:27:58.320
which was seen as a bad luck omen.  
 
On the 20th of January 1936, King George V,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:58.320 --> 01:28:06.560
passed away from chronic bronchitis, and his&nbsp;
popular eldest son, Edward, the Prince of Wales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:06.560 --> 01:28:14.080
succeeded to the throne to became King Edward&nbsp;
VIII, starting a brief reign that would be marred&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:14.080 --> 01:28:21.440
with controversy and disapproval towards his love&nbsp;
life, a situation which would haunt Elizabeth’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:21.440 --> 01:28:29.200
life forever. As Prince of Wales, Edward had&nbsp;
started a friendship with an American divorcee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:29.200 --> 01:28:36.480
Wallis Simpson. He was known to be deeply in&nbsp;
love with her. While the relationship was widely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:36.480 --> 01:28:42.640
reported in the United States and in European&nbsp;
newspapers, it was largely kept out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:42.640 --> 01:28:49.760
British press by arm-twisting by the government&nbsp;
of the day. The Prime Minister Stanley Baldwin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:49.760 --> 01:28:55.360
had attempted to persuade the King to give up&nbsp;
the relationship and explored the possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:55.360 --> 01:29:04.560
of a Morganatic marriage whereby Wallis would not&nbsp;
become queen, and any children would not accede.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:04.560 --> 01:29:10.640
This was ultimately rejected by Baldwin and the&nbsp;
prime ministers of Australia, South Africa, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:10.640 --> 01:29:19.360
Canada. Ultimately Edward VIII chose to abdicate,&nbsp;
addressing the nation by radio to say that he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:19.360 --> 01:29:27.040
found it ‘impossible to carry the heavy burden of&nbsp;
responsibility and to discharge my duties as King,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:27.040 --> 01:29:33.760
as I would wish to do, without the help and&nbsp;
support of the woman I love.’ On the 10th of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:33.760 --> 01:29:41.520
December 1936, in front of Albert and their two&nbsp;
other brothers, Edward signed the Instrument of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:41.520 --> 01:29:51.360
Abdication. Thereafter he flew to the continent&nbsp;
where he eventually married Wallis Simpson.
&nbsp;

01:29:51.360 --> 01:29:59.840
On the 11th of December 1936, the man who was&nbsp;
never meant to be king, Prince Albert became&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:59.840 --> 01:30:07.360
King George VI, and Elizabeth became Queen&nbsp;
Consort. Their coronation took place on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:07.360 --> 01:30:15.120
12th of May 1937, the same day that Edward&nbsp;
was due to have been crowned. Elizabeth was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:15.120 --> 01:30:21.600
now Queen of the United Kingdom and the British&nbsp;
Dominions, as well as the Empress of India. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:21.600 --> 01:30:28.960
scandal regarding Edward VIII was far from over&nbsp;
however. While Elizabeth was still supportive of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:28.960 --> 01:30:33.760
her husband’s decision to allow Edward to&nbsp;
create his elder brother Duke of Windsor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:33.760 --> 01:30:40.560
and to retain the title of Royal Highness, on&nbsp;
the advice of the cabinet Wallis was not to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:40.560 --> 01:30:47.840
given this title, a decision which was said to&nbsp;
have saddened the new King. However there was no&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:47.840 --> 01:30:55.600
love lost between Elizabeth and Wallis. Wallis&nbsp;
reportedly referred to Elizabeth as ‘Cookie’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:55.600 --> 01:31:01.920
believing that she was similar in appearance to&nbsp;
a fat Scottish cook, and Elizabeth was known to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:01.920 --> 01:31:08.480
simply call Wallis ‘that woman’. Elizabeth&nbsp;
remained bitter that the abdication had&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:08.480 --> 01:31:16.400
forced her husband onto the throne reluctantly and&nbsp;
unprepared. She also remained convinced that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:16.400 --> 01:31:24.320
events had ultimately shortened her husband’s&nbsp;
life, leaving her a relatively young widow.  
&nbsp;

01:31:24.320 --> 01:31:28.240
 
 
In the Spring of 1938,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:28.240 --> 01:31:37.280
the political climate across Europe was tense, and&nbsp;
talk of war was common. A royal tour of France was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:37.280 --> 01:31:45.200
organised to cement relations between Britain and&nbsp;
France in the face of potential German aggression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:45.200 --> 01:31:53.440
Five days before the royal couple’s departure the&nbsp;
Queen’s mother, the Countess of Strathmore died.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:53.440 --> 01:31:59.760
The trip to France was postponed and the court&nbsp;
went into three week’s mourning and dressed in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:59.760 --> 01:32:09.120
black, to be replaced after a few weeks by grey&nbsp;
and then mauve after that. The queen’s couturier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:09.120 --> 01:32:16.320
Norman Hartnell, had already devised a wardrobe&nbsp;
for the trip to France in pastel colours and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:16.320 --> 01:32:23.280
floral patterns which was now regarded as&nbsp;
entirely inappropriate. Thinking on his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:23.280 --> 01:32:30.640
feet he came up with what’s become known as the&nbsp;
White Wardrobe, white having been the colour of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:30.640 --> 01:32:38.320
deepest mourning among medieval queens. French&nbsp;
queens had traditionally worn white in mourning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:38.320 --> 01:32:44.080
and the King’s great-grandmother Queen&nbsp;
Victoria had been buried in white.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:44.080 --> 01:32:50.880
The team had two weeks to prepare the costumes&nbsp;
which were favourably received in France and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:50.880 --> 01:33:02.720
turned the Queen into an unlikely style-icon.
 
In March 1938, the German Chancellor Adolf Hitler&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:02.720 --> 01:33:09.200
annexed neighbouring Austria in a process&nbsp;
known as ‘The Anschluss.’ He then turned his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:09.200 --> 01:33:15.760
attention to Czechoslovakia where around three&nbsp;
million people in the Sudetenland region spoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:15.760 --> 01:33:22.720
German. Neither Britain nor France felt ready&nbsp;
to defend Czechoslovakia against the Germans&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:22.720 --> 01:33:30.080
and were anxious to avoid military intervention&nbsp;
at any cost. Both countries believed that peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:30.080 --> 01:33:37.600
could only be maintained by making territorial&nbsp;
concessions. The British Prime Minister Neville&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:37.600 --> 01:33:44.640
Chamberlain proposed a four-power conference&nbsp;
in Munich to discuss the situation at which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:44.640 --> 01:33:52.240
Hitler promised to find peaceful means to resolve&nbsp;
differences within Europe. Chamberlain returned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:52.240 --> 01:33:59.360
London pledging ‘peace in our time.’ The Royal&nbsp;
Family and the Prime Minister had always had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:59.360 --> 01:34:06.800
close relationship, and after the signing of the&nbsp;
Munich Agreement on the 30th of September 1938,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:06.800 --> 01:34:12.080
Neville Chamberlain joined the King and&nbsp;
Elizabeth on the balcony of Buckingham Palace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:12.080 --> 01:34:19.760
the first Prime minister to ever do this,&nbsp;
and received rapturous cheers from the crowd.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:19.760 --> 01:34:27.680
This sense of unity and patriotism was to be&nbsp;
short-lived. Chamberlain and the actions he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:27.680 --> 01:34:34.160
took against Hitler may have been popular&nbsp;
with the public, but politically this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:34.160 --> 01:34:39.680
regarded as a different matter. Elizabeth&nbsp;
had been wholly supportive of Chamberlain&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:39.680 --> 01:34:46.160
and had agreed that Hitler must be dealt with&nbsp;
delicately, but the House of Commons were highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:46.160 --> 01:34:53.760
critical of Chamberlain’s solution. 
 
Following the 1938 Munich Agreement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:53.760 --> 01:35:01.040
both Britain and France quickly moved to rearm&nbsp;
with many believing that war with Germany was now&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:01.040 --> 01:35:09.040
inevitable. From May to June that year King George&nbsp;
and Elizabeth embarked on a tour of Canada and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:09.040 --> 01:35:17.200
United States. This was the first-time reigning&nbsp;
monarchs had embarked on a tour of the country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:17.200 --> 01:35:23.600
During the trip, a veteran from the Boer War&nbsp;
asked Elizabeth whether she considered herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:23.600 --> 01:35:31.120
Scottish or English, to which she replied&nbsp;
that she was a Canadian. This response and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:31.120 --> 01:35:38.320
the tour in general were extremely successful and&nbsp;
changed the public perception of King George and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:38.320 --> 01:35:45.920
Elizabeth many of whom had believed the couple to&nbsp;
be less suitable to royal life than Edward VIII.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:45.920 --> 01:35:53.040
Speaking to the then Canadian prime minister,&nbsp;
William Lyon Mackenzie King, Elizabeth expressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:53.040 --> 01:36:00.320
her belief that the Canadian tour was the making&nbsp;
of George and Elizabeth as King and Queen, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:00.320 --> 01:36:07.600
that she would continue to return to Canada often&nbsp;
throughout the rest of her life, whether that be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:07.600 --> 01:36:12.320
visit for work or pleasure.  
 
 
&nbsp;

01:36:12.320 --> 01:36:19.200
This trans-Atlantic tour was seen not only as&nbsp;
an opportunity to assert British dominion over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:19.200 --> 01:36:27.280
Canada, but also as a means of ensuring American&nbsp;
support and involvement in the event of war in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:27.280 --> 01:36:33.840
Europe. While in the United States the royal&nbsp;
couple spent some time with President Franklin&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:33.840 --> 01:36:39.760
D. Roosevelt, and the First Lady, Eleanor&nbsp;
Roosevelt, even staying with them at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:39.760 --> 01:36:46.720
White House and their residence in the Hudson&nbsp;
Valley. The American First Lady reportedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:46.720 --> 01:36:54.160
spoke highly of Elizabeth and considered her&nbsp;
a suitable and capable monarch and Queen. 
 
&nbsp;

01:36:55.280 --> 01:37:04.240
By the early part of 1939 Hitler had become&nbsp;
determined to invade Poland. In a cat’s-cradle&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:04.240 --> 01:37:11.280
of alliances, Britain and France had pledged&nbsp;
to support Poland if it should be attacked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:11.280 --> 01:37:20.480
by Germany. Meanwhile, in secret negotiations,&nbsp;
Hitler signed a non-aggression pact with Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:20.480 --> 01:37:30.080
agreeing to share Poland after the invasion. The&nbsp;
scene was set for war. Germany invaded Poland on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:30.080 --> 01:37:38.720
the 31st of August 1939 prompted by a false&nbsp;
flag attack and a day later Britain declared&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:38.720 --> 01:37:47.280
war on Germany. A massive evacuation plan was&nbsp;
ordered to take civilians, mainly children, away&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:47.280 --> 01:37:55.760
from the cities and ports in an operation called&nbsp;
Pied Piper. Public air raid shelters were built,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:55.760 --> 01:38:02.720
with millions of so-called Anderson Shelters set&nbsp;
up in people’s gardens. After the surrender of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:02.720 --> 01:38:11.760
France in June 1940, England remained as Germany’s&nbsp;
sole enemy, and Hitler planned to undertake an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:11.760 --> 01:38:18.960
amphibious invasion ordering the Luftwaffe to&nbsp;
establish complete dominance in the skies over&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:18.960 --> 01:38:27.200
England in preparation. War came to London in&nbsp;
earnest on the 7th of September when hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:27.200 --> 01:38:34.640
of German bombers and fighter planes descended&nbsp;
on London in what became known as the Blitz.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:34.640 --> 01:38:42.000
The operation lasted for 57 nights in a row.&nbsp;
The targets for the bombers were principally&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:42.000 --> 01:38:49.680
in the East End of London and the docks. But very&nbsp;
few parts of the capital escaped, with Buckingham&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:49.680 --> 01:38:58.560
Palace bombed on at least nine occasions.
The King and Queen refused to leave the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:58.560 --> 01:39:06.480
country during the blitz despite the inherent&nbsp;
dangers and as obvious targets for enemy action.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:06.480 --> 01:39:13.440
Elizabeth insisted also that her daughters should&nbsp;
remain too, against the advice that they should&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:13.440 --> 01:39:20.560
be sent to Canada. In a letter to Queen Mary,&nbsp;
Elizabeth described one bomb attack on the Palace&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:20.560 --> 01:39:27.360
which saw a direct hit in the quadrangle, and&nbsp;
the destruction of the palace chapel. She spoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:27.360 --> 01:39:34.400
of her pride in the behaviour of the servants.&nbsp;
Just a short time later, in an act which was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:34.400 --> 01:39:41.360
enamour the King and Queen to the British people,&nbsp;
she visited a bomb site in West Ham where she&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:41.360 --> 01:39:50.160
spoke of ‘walking in a dead city, all the houses&nbsp;
evacuated, and yet through the broken windows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:50.160 --> 01:39:58.400
one saw all the poor little possessions,&nbsp;
photographs, beds, just as they were left.’&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:58.400 --> 01:40:05.360
During the Blitz, the Royal couple were seen&nbsp;
as key in keeping up morale and helping the war&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:05.360 --> 01:40:12.320
effort. They came to be seen as the embodiment&nbsp;
of the struggle against fascism and as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:12.320 --> 01:40:20.320
reminder of what the British were fighting for.&nbsp;
Rejecting the advice to flee to safety abroad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:20.320 --> 01:40:28.080
Elizabeth was quoted as saying, ‘The children&nbsp;
will not leave unless I do. I shall not leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:28.080 --> 01:40:35.480
unless their father does, and the King will not&nbsp;
leave the country in any circumstances.’
 
&nbsp;

01:40:35.480 --> 01:40:39.840
 
Queen Elizabeth contributed to the war effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:39.840 --> 01:40:47.520
in many other ways. For example, she sponsored&nbsp;
the publication of The Queen’s Book of the Red&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:47.520 --> 01:40:56.160
Cross. Appearing in November 1939, this book aimed&nbsp;
to help the funding for the Red Cross during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:56.160 --> 01:41:04.800
war. Its authors included A.A. Milne, who wrote&nbsp;
the Winnie the Pooh stories, the novelist Daphne&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:04.800 --> 01:41:13.040
du Maurier, and the poet T.S. Eliot, and included&nbsp;
photography by a close friend of the royal family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:13.040 --> 01:41:20.720
Cecil Beaton, along with artwork by Arthur&nbsp;
Wragg. Using her position and role as a caring&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:20.720 --> 01:41:28.160
and doting mother, Elizabeth would also take part&nbsp;
in radio broadcasts and try to comfort and help&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:28.160 --> 01:41:33.680
encourage families that had been separated&nbsp;
during the war, whether it was children who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:33.680 --> 01:41:42.080
had been evacuated to the countryside, or&nbsp;
fathers who were fighting overseas.  
 
&nbsp;

01:41:42.080 --> 01:41:47.040
Many members of the staff working at&nbsp;
Buckingham Palace were fighting in the army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:47.040 --> 01:41:53.360
so Elizabeth and her husband would spend their&nbsp;
days at Buckingham Palace, but then leave for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:53.360 --> 01:42:00.960
Windsor Castle later in the day. This was both&nbsp;
for their own personal safety and to be closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:00.960 --> 01:42:07.840
to their family. Elizabeth was always known&nbsp;
for being an extremely hard-working royal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:07.840 --> 01:42:16.080
and this was evident even during the Second World&nbsp;
War. Elizabeth would visit troops, factories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:16.080 --> 01:42:22.560
hospitals, and any other places that were&nbsp;
targeted by the German Luftwaffe. She also paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:22.560 --> 01:42:30.320
numerous visits to the East End of London. Despite&nbsp;
Elizabeth’s best intentions, these planned visits&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:30.320 --> 01:42:37.680
did not always go down well with the crowd.&nbsp;
Elizabeth was sometimes met with hostility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:37.680 --> 01:42:44.240
especially regarding her fashion choices in&nbsp;
a time of war. The people of the East End&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:44.240 --> 01:42:51.360
found Elizabeth’s choice of brightly coloured and&nbsp;
clearly expensive clothes to be distasteful, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:51.360 --> 01:42:59.120
a clear representation of the separation between&nbsp;
Elizabeth and them. However, Elizabeth and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:59.120 --> 01:43:07.280
stylist, Norman Hartnell, had deliberately chosen&nbsp;
to not wear black, as it was felt that black was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:07.280 --> 01:43:14.160
reminiscent of funerals and could be seen as&nbsp;
a bleak and depressing colour. She regarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:14.160 --> 01:43:21.760
the colours she wore as a token of hope. After&nbsp;
Buckingham Palace was bombed, Elizabeth stated&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:21.760 --> 01:43:27.440
that she was almost relieved as she believed that&nbsp;
she could now be seen as an equal to those living&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:27.440 --> 01:43:34.400
in the East End. Elizabeth's actions and sense&nbsp;
of duty during the war, and the significance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:34.400 --> 01:43:43.520
of her being a wartime queen, remains one of&nbsp;
the things she is fondly remembered for. 
 
&nbsp;

01:43:43.520 --> 01:43:50.240
Édouard Daladier, the French wartime prime&nbsp;
minister, held Elizabeth in high esteem and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:50.240 --> 01:43:56.880
was impressed by her conduct as queen and the&nbsp;
support and encouragement she gave to her husband&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:56.880 --> 01:44:05.040
and the people of her country. It’s believed that&nbsp;
Hitler believed the same, describing her as ‘the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:05.040 --> 01:44:12.800
most dangerous woman in Europe,’ and describing&nbsp;
her popularity as a threat to German interests.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:13.600 --> 01:44:21.040
And so, despite her wariness and disapproval of&nbsp;
the conflict, many historians regard her character&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:21.040 --> 01:44:29.520
and actions as key and central to the winning&nbsp;
of the Second World War, as was the strong bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:29.520 --> 01:44:37.120
that developed between her, the King and Winston&nbsp;
Churchill, who became Prime Minister after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:37.120 --> 01:44:42.760
resignation of Neville Chamberlain in May 1940.  
 
 
&nbsp;

01:44:43.360 --> 01:44:50.080
The Second World War came to an end on&nbsp;
the 2nd of September 1945, In the general&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:50.080 --> 01:44:55.600
election that followed shortly afterwards,&nbsp;
there was a surprise defeat for Britain’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:55.600 --> 01:45:02.720
wartime leader Winston Churchill, and a Labour&nbsp;
government was returned under Clement Atlee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:02.720 --> 01:45:11.040
triggering a period of considerable social change,&nbsp;
most notably the creation of the Welfare State.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:11.040 --> 01:45:15.920
The political views of the British Royal&nbsp;
family, in the past century at least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:15.920 --> 01:45:22.000
have been a closely guarded secret and whilst&nbsp;
it can never be fully known where Elizabeth’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:22.000 --> 01:45:28.880
true political beliefs and alliances lay, many&nbsp;
historians do believe that she was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:28.880 --> 01:45:35.280
most supportive Conservative monarchs.&nbsp;
However, it is rumoured that Elizabeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:35.280 --> 01:45:40.800
had told her close friend, the British&nbsp;
politician and journalist Woodrow Wyatt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:40.800 --> 01:45:46.960
that she liked and was fond of the Labour&nbsp;
Party, despite the popular historical belief&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:46.960 --> 01:45:54.880
that Elizabeth thought that only the poor and&nbsp;
uneducated voted for Labour. Elizabeth had also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:54.880 --> 01:46:01.360
told her family member, the Duchess of Grafton,&nbsp;
that she actually loved the communists. 
 
&nbsp;

01:46:03.600 --> 01:46:10.400
However, Elizabeth wasn’t always the&nbsp;
perfect royal. Whilst the King, Elizabeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:10.400 --> 01:46:17.760
and their children were touring South Africa in&nbsp;
1947, Elizabeth mistook one of their admirer's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:17.760 --> 01:46:25.040
excitement at seeing the royal family as an&nbsp;
act of attempted violence, and as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:25.040 --> 01:46:31.840
hit him with her umbrella from the royal car.&nbsp;
However, this is regarded as the only incident&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:31.840 --> 01:46:40.480
of Elizabeth breaking royal protocol and acting&nbsp;
unprofessionally. This same year, 1947, saw her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:40.480 --> 01:46:48.960
eldest daughter, Princess Elizabeth, marry Prince&nbsp;
Philip, a former Prince of Greece and Denmark.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:48.960 --> 01:46:56.480
Philip had been corresponding with her daughter&nbsp;
since she was 13 years of age in 1939, and after&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:56.480 --> 01:47:04.880
serving in the Royal Navy during the war asked the&nbsp;
King for his daughter’s hand in marriage in 1946.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:04.880 --> 01:47:11.920
The King consented but asked for the engagement&nbsp;
to be delayed until Princess Elizabeth’s 21st&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:11.920 --> 01:47:20.720
birthday. The ceremony took place at Westminster&nbsp;
Abbey on the 20th of November 1947.  
 
&nbsp;

01:47:21.920 --> 01:47:27.840
Some biographers have suggested that Elizabeth and&nbsp;
other members of the royal family had disapproved&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:27.840 --> 01:47:34.240
of Philip’s relationship with Princess Elizabeth,&nbsp;
partially because his sisters had married German&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:34.240 --> 01:47:42.400
noblemen who had Nazi links, but also because he&nbsp;
was without financial means. His family had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:42.400 --> 01:47:48.080
exiled from Greece after his uncle was forced&nbsp;
to abdicate for military failures during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:48.080 --> 01:47:56.880
Greco-Turkish war in 1922. Elizabeth is believed&nbsp;
to have initially teased Philip by giving him the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:56.880 --> 01:48:04.640
nickname, ‘The Hun,’ though later conceded that&nbsp;
he was indeed an English Gentleman. Following the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:04.640 --> 01:48:10.720
wedding, The King and Queen were due to embark on&nbsp;
another royal tour of Australia and New Zealand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:10.720 --> 01:48:19.760
in 1948, but the trip had to be delayed due&nbsp;
to the King’s deteriorating health.
 
 
&nbsp;

01:48:19.760 --> 01:48:20.480
 
In their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:20.480 --> 01:48:28.000
public broadcast to the nation in April 1948,&nbsp;
celebrating their silver wedding anniversary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:28.000 --> 01:48:35.680
the King thanked his consort Elizabeth and spoke&nbsp;
very highly of her and of her constant support&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:35.680 --> 01:48:43.280
throughout their marriage. That year George VI&nbsp;
had to stop public appearances for a period of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:43.280 --> 01:48:51.200
time because of a pain that he felt in his right&nbsp;
leg and foot, one that the doctors believed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:51.200 --> 01:49:00.080
Thromboangiitis Obliterans, also known as Buerger&nbsp;
Disease. His case, where his arteries had become&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:00.080 --> 01:49:07.760
severely hardened and had become swollen, was&nbsp;
so severe that amputation had been considered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:07.760 --> 01:49:16.400
On the 12th of March 1949, the King endured a&nbsp;
procedure known as a right lumbar sympathectomy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:16.400 --> 01:49:23.360
an injection given in his leg to relieve him of&nbsp;
his pain by blocking the sympathetic nerves in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:23.360 --> 01:49:30.400
the leg. While both the King and Elizabeth were&nbsp;
dealing with these issues surrounding his health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:30.400 --> 01:49:38.480
they also became grandparents on the 14th of&nbsp;
November 1948 when Prince Charles was born to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:38.480 --> 01:49:44.080
Princess Elizabeth and Prince Philip.&nbsp;
Throughout the remainder of her life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:44.080 --> 01:49:50.400
Elizabeth would share an incredibly close&nbsp;
bond with her grandson and was central to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:50.400 --> 01:49:55.920
encouraging him in his passion&nbsp;
for the arts and culture.  
 
&nbsp;

01:49:57.040 --> 01:50:02.800
Even though King George VI appeared to be&nbsp;
recovering and seemed to be in better health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:02.800 --> 01:50:09.920
it was Princess Elizabeth and Prince Philip who&nbsp;
embarked on the royal tour of Canada in place of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:09.920 --> 01:50:19.600
the King and Queen in October 1951, the Princess’s&nbsp;
first tour of Canada. This was a year after the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:19.600 --> 01:50:27.760
birth of their daughter, Princess Anne on the 15th&nbsp;
of August 1950. The King’s last public appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:27.760 --> 01:50:35.360
with his wife Elizabeth was in May 1951, when they&nbsp;
opened the Festival of Britain together in front&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:35.360 --> 01:50:43.680
of an audience of 3,000 people. This festival was&nbsp;
aimed at restoring patriotism and pride in being&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:43.680 --> 01:50:51.840
British, as well as being a celebration of the end&nbsp;
of rationing and victory in the Second World War.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:51.840 --> 01:50:59.200
However, just a few months later on the&nbsp;
23rd of September 1951, the King underwent a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:59.200 --> 01:51:06.320
pneumonectomy, the removal of his left lung at&nbsp;
home in Buckingham Palace after a lung cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:06.320 --> 01:51:14.720
diagnosis which was due to his heavy smoking.&nbsp;
By December the King had somewhat recovered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:14.720 --> 01:51:21.360
although in his Christmas Day address on the&nbsp;
25th of December 1951, it was clear that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:21.360 --> 01:51:28.800
speech sounded slower and his voice was deeper&nbsp;
than usual, although this was most likely due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:28.800 --> 01:51:35.840
the removal of his left recurrent laryngeal nerve&nbsp;
during the surgery, which can affect the sound of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:35.840 --> 01:51:39.120
one’s voice. 
 
 
&nbsp;

01:51:39.120 --> 01:51:45.360
Whilst the King was attempting to make a full&nbsp;
recovery in the hope of resuming his royal duties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:45.360 --> 01:51:50.880
his daughter Princess Elizabeth and her&nbsp;
husband Philip embarked on another royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:50.880 --> 01:51:59.200
tour of the Commonwealth, this time taking in&nbsp;
Kenya, New Zealand and Australia starting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:59.200 --> 01:52:07.200
January 1952. Before they left for this royal&nbsp;
tour, Albert had decided to wave them off at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:07.200 --> 01:52:15.600
London airport before their departure, despite&nbsp;
medical advice not to. This was the last time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:15.600 --> 01:52:23.360
that Elizabeth would ever see her father, and&nbsp;
it was the King’s last ever public appearance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:23.360 --> 01:52:29.600
The British public had been kept in the dark&nbsp;
about the health battle facing the King, and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:29.600 --> 01:52:37.600
for many it was a shock to see George VI looking&nbsp;
so ill on the newsreels in the cinema. On the 6th&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:37.600 --> 01:52:47.680
of February 1952, King George VI passed away in&nbsp;
his sleep. The royal physician Dr. James Ansell&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:47.680 --> 01:52:54.960
declared that the King had died from a coronary&nbsp;
thrombosis. It was believed that Ansell had not&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:54.960 --> 01:53:02.160
been aware of the King’s cancer diagnosis due to&nbsp;
the level of privacy surrounding the royal family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:02.160 --> 01:53:10.480
Indeed, the actual cause of the King’s death was&nbsp;
never actually made public. The press reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:10.480 --> 01:53:18.080
that the King died from a blood clot in his&nbsp;
heart. Princess Elizabeth was given the news of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:18.080 --> 01:53:25.760
her father’s death while she was at the Treetops&nbsp;
Resort in Kenya by her husband Prince Philip on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:25.760 --> 01:53:35.760
6th of February 1952. She was now Queen Elizabeth,&nbsp;
and her mother was to be known henceforth as Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:35.760 --> 01:53:43.280
Majesty Queen Elizabeth, the Queen Mother, a&nbsp;
title by which she is best known today.
 
&nbsp;

01:53:44.560 --> 01:53:50.720
Elizabeth initially struggled with the death&nbsp;
of her husband and chose to retire from royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:50.720 --> 01:54:00.000
life and duties. Not only had she lost a husband,&nbsp;
but also her role in public life, along with her&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:00.000 --> 01:54:08.880
husband’s many royal houses – Buckingham Palace,&nbsp;
Sandringham in Norfolk and Balmoral in Scotland.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:08.880 --> 01:54:14.720
It was decided that she should move out&nbsp;
of Buckingham Palace into Clarence House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:14.720 --> 01:54:22.160
a modest residence which it’s understood&nbsp;
she disliked intensely, but she also chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:22.160 --> 01:54:29.760
to buy a house on the west coast of Scotland as a&nbsp;
retirement home. It was the Prime Minister Winston&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:29.760 --> 01:54:37.840
Churchill who visited her that summer in Balmoral&nbsp;
to urge her not to retreat from public life as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:37.840 --> 01:54:45.760
Queen Victoria had done after the death of her&nbsp;
beloved husband Albert. Churchill was keen that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:45.760 --> 01:54:54.560
she should be on hand to support her daughter&nbsp;
as she navigated her new role as Queen.
 
&nbsp;

01:54:55.680 --> 01:55:03.440
Subsequently, she resumed royal duties, attending&nbsp;
functions and openings, and flew to the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:03.440 --> 01:55:10.160
States to receive a cheque for the George VI&nbsp;
memorial fund. She became a patron to more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:10.160 --> 01:55:18.720
300 organisations and made history by becoming&nbsp;
the first female chancellor of London University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:18.720 --> 01:55:25.680
and the first female Lord Warden and&nbsp;
Admiral of the Cinque Ports. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:25.680 --> 01:55:32.960
her principal role was very much as a supportive&nbsp;
and encouraging influence on her daughters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:32.960 --> 01:55:40.560
The Queen Mother remained especially close to her&nbsp;
daughter Margaret, and due to their special bond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:40.560 --> 01:55:46.960
they both embarked on a royal tour of the&nbsp;
Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland, which later&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:46.960 --> 01:55:56.880
became Zimbabwe, Zambia and Malawi from the 1st of&nbsp;
July to the 16th of July 1953. This was the Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:56.880 --> 01:56:03.840
Mother’s first international visit since the state&nbsp;
funeral of her husband on the 14th of February&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:03.840 --> 01:56:12.320
1952 at Westminster Hall. Even though this royal&nbsp;
tour was originally scheduled to be carried out by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:12.320 --> 01:56:19.200
Princess Elizabeth, the tour was regarded as&nbsp;
a great success. The visit to the Federation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:19.200 --> 01:56:27.280
of Rhodesia and Nyasaland is regarded as a key&nbsp;
moment in her onward career as a royal figure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:27.280 --> 01:56:34.640
kicked off a new chapter in her royal life. During&nbsp;
this tour the Queen Mother placed the foundation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:34.640 --> 01:56:43.920
stone for the University College of Rhodesia and&nbsp;
Nyasaland, now the University of Zimbabwe. In 1957&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:43.920 --> 01:56:51.920
she became the university's president and would&nbsp;
often attend their events when possible.
 
 
&nbsp;

01:56:51.920 --> 01:56:59.040
Despite her support for education in Africa, there&nbsp;
are persistent suggestions that the Queen Mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:59.040 --> 01:57:06.640
held racist views, however, whether they went&nbsp;
beyond those prevalent in society at the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:06.640 --> 01:57:14.480
is uncertain. Some historians and biographers of&nbsp;
the Queen Mother report her support for racial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:14.480 --> 01:57:22.240
segregation, apartheid, and white supremacy in&nbsp;
Rhodesia. She has even been accused of supporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:22.240 --> 01:57:28.640
the empire and the British rule over African&nbsp;
countries which she believed were not able&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:28.640 --> 01:57:36.480
to rule themselves. On the other hand, a former&nbsp;
equerry to the Queen Mother, Major Colin Burgess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:36.480 --> 01:57:43.760
has denied the suggestion that the Queen Mother&nbsp;
ever made racial insults and slurs, although he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:43.760 --> 01:57:52.080
does report in his book, Behind Palace Doors,&nbsp;
that she was often ignorant of current affairs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:52.080 --> 01:57:59.520
Furthermore, the Queen Mother never publicly&nbsp;
voiced offensive views, if she indeed held them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:59.520 --> 01:58:05.520
and so it is difficult to prove the veracity&nbsp;
of the claims. The British historian and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:05.520 --> 01:58:11.840
former Conservative, Sir Robert Rhodes James,&nbsp;
has said that the Queen Mother had told him&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:11.840 --> 01:58:19.840
that she did not agree with discrimination&nbsp;
regarding race, and opposed apartheid.
 
&nbsp;

01:58:19.840 --> 01:58:26.960
While her daughter Queen Elizabeth II was finding&nbsp;
her feet, she still commanded enormous respect in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:26.960 --> 01:58:33.040
the royal household. At the start of the new&nbsp;
Queen’s first royal tour of the Commonwealth&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:33.040 --> 01:58:41.040
as monarch from 1953 to 1954, the Queen&nbsp;
Mother remained in Britain and acted as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:41.040 --> 01:58:47.840
the Counsellor of State. This meant that,&nbsp;
as a more senior member of the royal family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:47.840 --> 01:58:53.440
the Queen Mother carried out many of the&nbsp;
duties commonly done by the reigning monarch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:53.440 --> 01:59:02.160
such as signing official documents, and carrying&nbsp;
on royal business in the monarch’s absence.  
 
&nbsp;

01:59:02.160 --> 01:59:09.840
The Queen Mother’s fondness for Scotland&nbsp;
is undisputed. After being widowed in 1952,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:09.840 --> 01:59:16.720
she had brought the partially derelict Barrogill&nbsp;
Castle and its surrounding estate, and planned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:16.720 --> 01:59:24.960
to restore the building so it could be used as&nbsp;
her holiday home. From the years 1953 to 1960&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:24.960 --> 01:59:31.520
much work was done on the castle, including the&nbsp;
plastering and supply of electricity and water,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:31.520 --> 01:59:39.200
which was the first time the castle had had&nbsp;
these resources. From 1955 until her death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:39.200 --> 01:59:44.720
the Queen Mother would visit the Barrogill&nbsp;
Castle for three weeks in August and for almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:44.720 --> 01:59:53.200
a fortnight in October. She was said to visit this&nbsp;
castle as an opportunity for escapism and peace&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:53.200 --> 02:00:02.080
away from the distractions of life in London.
One such distraction was her youngest daughter&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:02.080 --> 02:00:08.080
Margaret's controversial relationship with former&nbsp;
Battle of Britain pilot, Group Captain Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:08.080 --> 02:00:17.040
Townsend. Townsend had served as King George VI’s&nbsp;
equerry, or assistant, from 1944 until the King’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:17.040 --> 02:00:24.560
death and had been kept on by the new queen. Peter&nbsp;
Townsend was also appointed as the comptroller to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:24.560 --> 02:00:31.280
the Queen Mother in 1952, which meant that he&nbsp;
was charged with overseeing the spending of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:31.280 --> 02:00:38.560
royal household. According to several sources,&nbsp;
Princess Margaret first met Peter Townsend when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:38.560 --> 02:00:46.560
she was fourteen, but the romance only began eight&nbsp;
years later, after the death of her father. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:46.560 --> 02:00:52.960
Group Captain was 16 years older than Margaret&nbsp;
and later confessed that he had been attracted&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:52.960 --> 02:01:00.640
to her intense beauty and sense of humour. The&nbsp;
relationship had been kept from the public eye,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:00.640 --> 02:01:06.480
but an off-guard moment at the Queen’s&nbsp;
coronation where Margaret was seen to pick some&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:06.480 --> 02:01:13.880
fluff of the Group Captain’s uniform, fuelled&nbsp;
speculation in the international press.
 
&nbsp;

02:01:13.880 --> 02:01:17.600
 
Townsend had served in the Indian&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:17.600 --> 02:01:24.720
Civil Service and when he returned to Britain&nbsp;
he’d married Cecil Rosemary Pratt, Marchioness&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:24.720 --> 02:01:33.840
of Camden after a short engagement and the couple&nbsp;
had had two sons. After his wife’s infidelity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:33.840 --> 02:01:41.440
he filed for divorce. It was his status as a&nbsp;
divorcee that made it impossible for him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:41.440 --> 02:01:48.880
marry Margaret as it was against the teachings of&nbsp;
the Church of England. Parliament was also firmly&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:48.880 --> 02:01:55.760
against the union. As Defender of the Faith, the&nbsp;
head of the Church of England, her sister the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:55.760 --> 02:02:03.600
queen was unable to sanction the relationship.&nbsp;
A compromise was proposed. Under the terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:03.600 --> 02:02:11.520
the Royal Marriages Act of 1772, no descendant&nbsp;
of George II could marry without the permission&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:11.520 --> 02:02:17.840
of the reigning monarch. It was suggested&nbsp;
that Margaret and Peter Townsend wait until&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:17.840 --> 02:02:26.400
the princess turned 25 years of age and was thus&nbsp;
deemed to be capable of making her own decisions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:26.400 --> 02:02:33.760
Townsend was posted to Brussels as an air attaché&nbsp;
at the British Embassy, but even by the time of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:33.760 --> 02:02:41.680
Margaret’s 25th birthday, the Queen had still&nbsp;
not given her approval for the marriage.  
 
&nbsp;

02:02:43.280 --> 02:02:49.760
Despite changing attitudes, and a lingering&nbsp;
sadness among the British public that Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:49.760 --> 02:02:58.800
VIII had been unable to marry for love and retain&nbsp;
his royal status, permission was still not given.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:58.800 --> 02:03:06.800
The Prime Minister of the time, Anthony Eden, was&nbsp;
himself a divorcee and had subsequently remarried,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:06.800 --> 02:03:14.640
and one might expect that he would be sympathetic&nbsp;
to the Princess's plight. Eden did amend the Royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:14.640 --> 02:03:23.120
Marriages Act, but in a way that was regarded as&nbsp;
cruel and unfair. The change would have allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:23.120 --> 02:03:30.240
Margaret and Townsend’s marriage, but the Princess&nbsp;
and their future children would be removed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:30.240 --> 02:03:37.440
the line of succession as a result. Whilst Eden&nbsp;
did highlight that Margaret’s removal from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:37.440 --> 02:03:45.200
succession would not affect her status as a royal&nbsp;
in any way, in the end Margaret chose not to marry&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:45.200 --> 02:03:53.600
Townsend. On the 31st of October 1955, Princess&nbsp;
Margaret made her position clear that she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:53.600 --> 02:03:59.920
mindful of the teachings of the church, and was&nbsp;
conscious of her duty to the Commonwealth, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:59.920 --> 02:04:07.440
that she had decided to put these considerations&nbsp;
before any others. Margaret was said to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:07.440 --> 02:04:15.840
heartbroken, but ultimately moved on.
Despite tension and friction between&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:15.840 --> 02:04:23.120
her daughters, the Queen Mother lived a rather&nbsp;
peaceful private life as a widow. Her love for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:23.120 --> 02:04:30.640
field sports and animals continued throughout her&nbsp;
life, and now as Queen Mother she had more time to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:30.640 --> 02:04:38.880
dedicate to these passions. A deep affection for&nbsp;
horses was a love shared between the whole family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:38.880 --> 02:04:45.920
as her daughter Queen Elizabeth II was also an&nbsp;
avid admirer, and she developed a new interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:45.920 --> 02:04:53.840
in steeplechasing, a race in which horses must&nbsp;
jump over various fences and terrain obstacles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:53.840 --> 02:05:00.160
This fascination with the sport came as a result&nbsp;
of her friendship with Anthony Bingham Mildmay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:00.160 --> 02:05:07.280
the second Baron Mildmay of Flete, who was an&nbsp;
amateur steeplechaser. He sat next to the Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:07.280 --> 02:05:13.520
Mother at a dinner in Windsor Palace in 1949,&nbsp;
where he struck up a conversation with the then&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:13.520 --> 02:05:19.760
queen, in which he recommended that Elizabeth&nbsp;
should buy a horse that she could share with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:19.760 --> 02:05:26.160
her daughter Princess Elizabeth. With the&nbsp;
help of Mildmay’s trainer, Peter Cazalet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:26.160 --> 02:05:33.920
the horse Monaveen, was selected for the royals,&nbsp;
and this horse won its first race at the Fontwell&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:33.920 --> 02:05:42.080
Park Racecourse in October 1949, where it won&nbsp;
the Chichester Handicap Race. The Queen Mother’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:42.080 --> 02:05:50.720
horses won 500 steeplechase races, and she was&nbsp;
an avid follower of every steeplechase race,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:50.720 --> 02:05:56.480
even having the commentaries piped directly&nbsp;
to her place of residence in Clarence House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:56.480 --> 02:06:03.480
although it is unclear whether she actually&nbsp;
placed bets on these races or not.  
 
&nbsp;

02:06:03.480 --> 02:06:07.200
 
As well as a love for horses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:07.200 --> 02:06:15.440
the Queen Mother also enjoyed angling. In 1982,&nbsp;
after nearly choking on a fish bone and having&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:15.440 --> 02:06:21.760
to have the stuck bone removed surgically&nbsp;
at Aberdeen Royal Infirmary, she joked that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:21.760 --> 02:06:28.400
that incident was simply an act of revenge by&nbsp;
the salmon she had caught. Another lifelong&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:28.400 --> 02:06:35.600
interest was collecting various artworks. Whilst&nbsp;
she acquired most of her large collection during&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:35.600 --> 02:06:42.160
her time as reigning monarch, she was always&nbsp;
continually adding to her collection of art and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:42.160 --> 02:06:50.240
often developed a close bond with her favourite&nbsp;
artists, such as John Piper or Edward Seago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:50.240 --> 02:06:57.680
She also had a liking for artwork that had been&nbsp;
inspired by previous royals, such as the sketches&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:57.680 --> 02:07:04.800
made by the Scottish painter Sir David Wilkie for&nbsp;
his portrait of Queen Victoria in her youth. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:04.800 --> 02:07:12.800
was also supportive of artists who had been active&nbsp;
in the Second World War. Among many other works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:12.800 --> 02:07:22.480
her collection included paintings by Monet and&nbsp;
Augustus John and a number of Fabergé Eggs.
 
&nbsp;

02:07:22.480 --> 02:07:29.120
It was not long before the love life of&nbsp;
Elizabeth’s daughter Margaret took centre stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:29.120 --> 02:07:37.200
again. In the late fifties, she embarked on an&nbsp;
initially scandalous affair with a photographer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:37.200 --> 02:07:45.120
Antony Armstrong-Jones. Once again, the liaison&nbsp;
was kept secret from the public and yet there&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:45.120 --> 02:07:50.320
are suggestions that the Queen Mother not&nbsp;
only knew about her daughter’s affair but&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:50.320 --> 02:07:58.080
actively encouraged it. At the time Margaret&nbsp;
shared Windsor’s Royal lodge with her mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:58.080 --> 02:08:03.680
and it’s been reported by biographers that the&nbsp;
Queen Mother grew fond of the photographer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:03.680 --> 02:08:10.880
whom she dubbed ‘Tony.’ Soon Margaret was&nbsp;
paying secret visits to Armstrong-Jones’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:10.880 --> 02:08:19.680
studio in Pimlico, and he was a frequent visitor&nbsp;
to the Lodge in Windsor. He did so under cover of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:19.680 --> 02:08:26.960
‘building and aviary’ for the Queen Mother.&nbsp;
In 1959, the Queen Mother threw a party to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:26.960 --> 02:08:34.000
welcome Princess Alexandra home from Australia,&nbsp;
at which it was clear that Margaret and Antony&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:34.000 --> 02:08:41.280
Armstrong-Jones had feelings for one another.&nbsp;
They would marry a year later in a ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:41.280 --> 02:08:50.240
which was the first of its kind to be televised.&nbsp;
On the 6th of October 1961, Armstrong-Jones was&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:50.240 --> 02:08:59.760
given the title of the Earl of Snowdon.  
On the 28th of May 1960, a few weeks after&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:59.760 --> 02:09:08.320
his marriage to Margaret, Armstrong Jones’s&nbsp;
illegitimate daughter, Polly Fry, was born.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:08.320 --> 02:09:14.320
She was subsequently brought up by a close&nbsp;
friend of the Snowdons, and it wasn’t until&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:14.320 --> 02:09:22.800
2004 that she persuaded Lord Snowdon to have&nbsp;
a DNA test which confirmed their link. From&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:22.800 --> 02:09:30.240
his marriage to Princess Margaret the Queen Mother&nbsp;
had two more grandchildren, David Armstrong-Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:30.240 --> 02:09:38.240
the second Earl of Snowdon on the 3rd of November&nbsp;
1961, and Lady Sarah Armstrong-Jones, now Lady&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:38.240 --> 02:09:48.080
Sarah Chatto on the 1st of May 1964. Nevertheless,&nbsp;
Margaret and Antony had a turbulent marriage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:48.080 --> 02:09:55.680
with widespread rumours of adultery and drug use.&nbsp;
While it’s accepted generally that Antony was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:55.680 --> 02:10:04.880
first to be unfaithful, Margaret went on to have&nbsp;
affairs of her own. The couple divorced in 1978&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:04.880 --> 02:10:11.760
in what was the first royal divorce since the&nbsp;
annulment of Henry VIII’s marriage to Anne of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:11.760 --> 02:10:19.440
Cleeves in 1540. The Queen Mother maintained&nbsp;
a close relationship with Armstrong-Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:19.440 --> 02:10:26.640
and it was believed that many members of the royal&nbsp;
family supported him after their divorce.  
 
&nbsp;

02:10:26.640 --> 02:10:30.080
 
Increasingly the Queen Mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:30.080 --> 02:10:39.600
encountered ill health. In February of 1964, she&nbsp;
underwent an emergency appendectomy, which led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:39.600 --> 02:10:46.000
the postponement of her planned royal tour of the&nbsp;
Commonwealth countries of Australia, New Zealand&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:46.000 --> 02:10:54.080
and Fiji for two years. After the procedure, the&nbsp;
Queen Mother recovered aboard the Britannia, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:54.080 --> 02:11:02.560
royal family’s cherished yacht, during a Caribbean&nbsp;
cruise. She would have two battles with cancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:02.560 --> 02:11:10.000
with the first in December 1966 when she underwent&nbsp;
an operation to remove a cancerous tumour after&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:10.000 --> 02:11:18.400
her colon cancer diagnosis. In 1984, the Queen&nbsp;
Mother was diagnosed with cancer for the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:18.400 --> 02:11:26.160
time. She was then given a diagnosis of breast&nbsp;
cancer, which resulted in the removal of a lump&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:26.160 --> 02:11:33.760
in her breast. The public was not made aware&nbsp;
of these health challenges at the time. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:33.760 --> 02:11:40.320
these issues with her health did not stop her from&nbsp;
undertaking her royal duties, and she continued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:40.320 --> 02:11:47.440
be one of the most hardworking royals for the&nbsp;
rest of her life. She embarked on over forty&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:47.440 --> 02:11:55.840
official royal overseas visits, notably visiting&nbsp;
Iran in 1975 at the invitation of the country’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:55.840 --> 02:12:04.160
last monarch, the Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi.&nbsp;
She was warmly received there and it was reported&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:04.160 --> 02:12:11.280
that many were amused by the fact that the Queen&nbsp;
Mother would engage in conversation with anyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:11.280 --> 02:12:19.840
regardless of their hierarchy or status. Despite&nbsp;
her busy schedule, time was always set aside for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:19.840 --> 02:12:28.640
leisure. Between 1976 and 1984, she would visit&nbsp;
France to spend her summer there every year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:28.640 --> 02:12:38.480
and she would embark on her own private trips&nbsp;
to other European countries up until 1992.  
 
&nbsp;

02:12:38.480 --> 02:12:46.960
In 1981, the Queen Mother’s favourite&nbsp;
grandson Charles married Lady Diana Spencer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:46.960 --> 02:12:54.480
Diana’s grandmother Ruth, Lady Fermoy, had been&nbsp;
one of the Queen Mother’s ladies-in- waiting since&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:54.480 --> 02:13:03.200
1956 and Diana blamed her for turning the Queen&nbsp;
Mother against her. Princess Diana once told a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:03.200 --> 02:13:09.600
friend in a secretly taped conversation that her&nbsp;
husband’s grandmother was ‘always looking at me&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:09.600 --> 02:13:18.560
with a strange look in her eyes.’ She described&nbsp;
it as interest and pity mixed in one. One issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:18.560 --> 02:13:25.440
that Diana reportedly found particularly galling&nbsp;
was how the Queen Mother was demonstratively&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:25.440 --> 02:13:33.520
preferential towards her eldest son, Prince&nbsp;
William over her younger son Harry. Later&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:33.520 --> 02:13:39.920
documentaries would claim that the Queen Mother&nbsp;
would invite Prince William over for tea and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:39.920 --> 02:13:47.440
invite Prince Harry. In later life, however,&nbsp;
she seems to have favoured Harry over William,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:47.440 --> 02:13:55.120
leaving him a larger share of her estate than his&nbsp;
elder brother. After Princess Diana’s death in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:55.120 --> 02:14:02.160
car crash in Paris in 1997, The Queen Mother was&nbsp;
said to have been upset at the criticism levelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:02.160 --> 02:14:09.160
at her daughter the Queen for being uncaring&nbsp;
and insensitive to the mood of the nation 
 
&nbsp;

02:14:09.160 --> 02:14:12.160
 
In 1987,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:12.160 --> 02:14:19.280
a major controversy was revealed surrounding the&nbsp;
Queen Mother, one that impacted her popularity&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:19.280 --> 02:14:28.640
to some degree. It was revealed that her nieces,&nbsp;
Nerissa Bowes-Lyon and Katherine Bowes-Lyon, had&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:28.640 --> 02:14:36.080
been wrongly listed as dead by Burke’s Peerage, a&nbsp;
yearly publication of aristocratic genealogy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:36.080 --> 02:14:49.840
heraldry, in 1963, claiming that Nerissa had died&nbsp;
in 1940 and Katherine in 1961. In 1987 the tabloid&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:49.840 --> 02:14:58.880
newspaper, The Sun, published an article proving&nbsp;
Burke’s Peerage as wrong, and instead informing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:58.880 --> 02:15:05.760
the public the Queen Mother’s nieces were instead&nbsp;
living in The Royal Earlswood Hospital for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:05.760 --> 02:15:15.200
with mental disabilities since 1941. Both were&nbsp;
classified as ‘imbeciles’ in the terminology of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:15.200 --> 02:15:23.120
the day. The paper revealed that neither Nerissa&nbsp;
nor Katherine had ever learned how to talk, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:23.120 --> 02:15:32.560
they had essentially been cut off and ignored by&nbsp;
their family, other than a yearly payment of £125&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:32.560 --> 02:15:42.640
to Earlswood Hospital. When Nerissa passed away in&nbsp;
1986, no family reportedly attended her funeral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:42.640 --> 02:15:48.080
There have been mixed accounts regarding this&nbsp;
controversial chapter in the Queen Mother’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:48.080 --> 02:15:57.600
life. A documentary produced by Channel 4&nbsp;
in 2011, titled The Queen’s Hidden Cousins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:57.600 --> 02:16:04.400
included claims made by nurses who worked at the&nbsp;
hospital at that time that no members of the royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:04.400 --> 02:16:14.000
family nor the Bowes-Lyon family ever visited the&nbsp;
girls. However, sources within the family argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:14.000 --> 02:16:22.160
otherwise. They maintain that the two sisters were&nbsp;
often visited by their mother Fenella Bowes-Lyon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:22.160 --> 02:16:30.800
the Queen Mother’s sister-in-law, until she died&nbsp;
in 1966. It has also been claimed that the Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:30.800 --> 02:16:37.600
Mother also began to develop a relationship with&nbsp;
her nieces after learning that they were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:37.600 --> 02:16:45.600
still alive in 1982, and would send them money&nbsp;
for presents at Christmas time and on each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:45.600 --> 02:16:53.120
their birthdays. There has been no official&nbsp;
confirmation of this. Throughout her life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:53.120 --> 02:17:01.120
the Queen Mother was heavily involved with charity&nbsp;
work and became patron of Mencap, supporting those&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:01.120 --> 02:17:11.120
with learning disabilities in 1986.  
 
On the 4th of August 1990, the Queen Mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:11.120 --> 02:17:17.920
celebrated her 90th birthday. The landmark&nbsp;
was commemorated publicly on the 27th of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:17.920 --> 02:17:24.880
June 1990 with a parade thanking her for&nbsp;
her service and endurance as one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:24.880 --> 02:17:33.359
longest and hardest working members of the royal&nbsp;
family. For her 100th birthday 10 years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:33.359 --> 02:17:40.960
another parade was hosted to celebrate her life&nbsp;
and achievements. Actors such as Sir John Mills&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:40.960 --> 02:17:48.560
and Sir Norman Wisdom participated, and the Royal&nbsp;
Bank of Scotland produced a special £20 bank note&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:48.560 --> 02:17:56.319
to celebrate this momentous occasion. However,&nbsp;
The Queen Mother’s health was quickly failing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:56.319 --> 02:18:03.759
although this did not prevent her from carrying&nbsp;
out royal duties. Despite injuring her pelvis in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:03.760 --> 02:18:10.319
December 2001, she refused to sit for&nbsp;
the national anthem for her husband’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:10.319 --> 02:18:19.840
memorial service on the 6th of February 2002 as&nbsp;
a mark of respect for her beloved husband.  
 
&nbsp;

02:18:21.040 --> 02:18:28.560
Tragedy struck for the Queen Mother on the&nbsp;
9th of February 2002, just three days after&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:28.560 --> 02:18:34.479
the anniversary of her husband's passing,&nbsp;
with the death of her youngest daughter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:34.479 --> 02:18:41.839
Princess Margaret after suffering from her fourth&nbsp;
stroke. Members of the royal family and the queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:41.840 --> 02:18:47.200
herself were concerned about the Queen Mother&nbsp;
making the journey from her place of residence&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:47.200 --> 02:18:54.880
in Norfolk to St George's Chapel in Windsor, where&nbsp;
Margaret was to be buried, but she flew there by&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:18:54.880 --> 02:19:01.680
helicopter, partially so no press would be able&nbsp;
to take photographs of her in her wheelchair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:01.680 --> 02:19:10.160
which she did not like to use. Her last public&nbsp;
appearance was on the 5th of March 2002,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:10.160 --> 02:19:17.280
when the Queen Mother attended a garden lunch&nbsp;
party for the Eton Beagles, a hunting society&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:17.280 --> 02:19:25.920
at the prestigious Eton School. 
 
On the 30th of March 2002, the Queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:25.920 --> 02:19:34.880
Mother passed away with her eldest daughter at her&nbsp;
side at the age of 101. At the time of her death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:34.880 --> 02:19:41.280
she was the longest living member of the British&nbsp;
royal family in history. Queen Elizabeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:41.280 --> 02:19:48.240
the Queen Mother was laid in state for three days&nbsp;
at Westminster Hall, and it's calculated that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:48.240 --> 02:19:58.479
200,000 people paid their respects to her there&nbsp;
before her funeral on the 9th of April 2002. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:19:58.479 --> 02:20:04.319
death was also marked in the Commonwealth,&nbsp;
with the then governor-general of Canada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:04.319 --> 02:20:10.880
Adrienne Clarkson, asking Canadian citizens&nbsp;
to remember the Queen Mother and honour their&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:10.880 --> 02:20:18.479
memory of her on the day of her funeral. The Queen&nbsp;
Mother was laid to rest beside her husband and&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:18.479 --> 02:20:26.559
their daughter Margaret in St George’s Chapel,&nbsp;
and after her funeral, her wreath was placed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:26.560 --> 02:20:34.800
top of the grave of the unknown soldier upon her&nbsp;
request, a repeat of her gesture at her wedding&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:34.800 --> 02:20:43.680
some 79 years before. The Queen Mother had a major&nbsp;
impact on Britain throughout her life and reign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:43.680 --> 02:20:51.200
and it was her stable and loving marriage with&nbsp;
King George VI and the support she provided him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:51.200 --> 02:20:58.880
that has often been credited as saving the British&nbsp;
monarchy in a time of immense social and political&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:20:58.880 --> 02:21:05.840
upheaval. Upon her death, the Queen Mother was&nbsp;
remembered as kind and funny, and was one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:05.840 --> 02:21:12.560
the more liked members of the royal family, and&nbsp;
this has only been furthered by her depiction in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:12.560 --> 02:21:20.240
the popular Netflix drama, The Crown.  
What do you think about Queen Elizabeth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:20.240 --> 02:21:26.479
the Queen Mother? Do you think she was the&nbsp;
saving grace of the British monarchy? Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:26.479 --> 02:21:34.479
were her rumoured racist views and neglect of her&nbsp;
nieces an insight into her true character? Please&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:34.479 --> 02:21:44.160
let us know in the comment section and in the&nbsp;
meantime, thank you very much for watching.
&nbsp;

02:21:47.120 --> 02:21:53.040
George VI and the Queen Mother were an unlikely&nbsp;
couple to become King and Queen of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:21:53.040 --> 02:22:00.880
Kingdom. The royal succession was meant to ensure&nbsp;
that King Edward VIII would rule for many years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:00.880 --> 02:22:10.319
but Edward’s determination to marry Wallis Simpson&nbsp;
dictated otherwise. For Britain it was probably a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:10.319 --> 02:22:17.359
serendipitous change. While Edward and Wallis&nbsp;
went on to court the Nazis within months of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:17.359 --> 02:22:24.719
his abdication, George began a reign that is very&nbsp;
well-regarded amongst Britain’s modern monarchs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:24.720 --> 02:22:31.280
offering moral leadership during the Second World&nbsp;
War and carving out a successful relationship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:31.280 --> 02:22:38.559
Winston Churchill as Britain’s wartime leader,&nbsp;
despite a less than promising start between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:38.560 --> 02:22:46.479
two men. Even before he became king, Elizabeth had&nbsp;
been a bedrock of support for the reserved younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:46.479 --> 02:22:53.040
son of George V, encouraging him to overcome the&nbsp;
speech impediment and other issues that had left&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:22:53.040 --> 02:23:00.479
him lacking in confidence as a young man. Their&nbsp;
time as king and queen was notable in other ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:00.479 --> 02:23:06.799
too. George and Elizabeth, for instance, will&nbsp;
forever have the distinction of being the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:06.800 --> 02:23:13.840
Emperor and Empress of India. Together they&nbsp;
navigated both personal struggles and profound&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:13.840 --> 02:23:20.160
political challenges, laying the groundwork&nbsp;
in the process for a new British monarchy that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:23:20.160 --> 02:23:27.760
would still have a great role to play in the world&nbsp;
even after the sun set on Britain’s empire.    

